iew":"visible","k":12,"source":"pgvector","top":[{"id":"c156","score":0.759},{"id":"c049","score":0.754},{"id":"c066","score":0.733},{"id":"c002","score":0.727},{"id":"c122","score":0.723},{"id":"c127","score":0.706},{"id":"c178","score":0.702},{"id":"c130","score":0.689},{"id":"c147","score":0.661},{"id":"c081","score":0.66}]}],"candidates":{"c156":{"id":"c156","name":"Ishita Nguyen","semantic":0.759240171411162,"seen":1,"coverage":{"m1":0.690389903857643,"m2":0.6522427497232146,"m3":0.6722444561973764,"n1":0.6916286533108881,"n2":0.6239845185420485},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":517,"end":613,"quote":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m2","start":403,"end":516,"quote":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m3","start":614,"end":719,"quote":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.93},{"requirementId":"n1","start":301,"end":402,"quote":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.9},{"requirementId":"n2","start":403,"end":516,"quote":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","strength":"partial","by":"claude","confidence":0.88}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_vzoa7p_c156","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","targets":["API design"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.","rubric":"A strong answer keeps the old field alongside the new one during a deprecation window, adds the new score under a new name rather than changing the meaning of the existing field, and uses versioning or response headers to signal deprecation. It also mentions tracking usage of the old field per client, notifying owners with a dated timeline, and removing the old behavior only after usage drops to zero or an agreed cutoff.","targets":["API design"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","rubric":"A good answer parses the input with JSON.parse, filters the array by checking that tags includes both required strings, maps to ids, and returns JSON.stringify of the result. It handles the empty case by returning \"[]\" and does not reorder elements.","targets":["API design"],"tests":[{"input":"[{\"id\":\"a\",\"tags\":[\"public\",\"indexed\"]},{\"id\":\"b\",\"tags\":[\"public\"]},{\"id\":\"c\",\"tags\":[\"indexed\",\"public\",\"x\"]}]","expected":"[\"a\",\"c\"]"},{"input":"[]","expected":"[]"},{"input":"[{\"id\":\"z\",\"tags\":[\"private\"]}]","expected":"[]"}]}],"emailSubject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","emailBody":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team","unverified":["API design"]},"sent":{"channel":"outbox","at":1793426298386},"responses":[{"kind":"short","answer":"I would use a POST or GET search endpoint with a typed request body: `query` (text or vector reference), `limit` (default 20, hard cap 100), `cursor` (opaque string, not a raw offset), and `filters` as an object with an explicit allowlist of fields and operators. The response returns `items` (each with `id`, `score`, and a `metadata` object), plus `next_cursor` (null at the end) and `schema_version`. Cursors encode the last sort key plus a snapshot marker so paging stays stable under concurrent writes. Filters are validated against a versioned schema: unknown fields are rejected with a 400 that names the field, not silently ignored, and new filterable fields are added as optional so older clients keep working. Response fields only grow additively, so clients that ignore unknown keys keep working as the schema evolves."},{"kind":"scenario","answer":"I would use an expand-and-contract rollout. For the field rename, I would publish the new name alongside the old one and have the service write both for a defined deprecation window, documenting the mapping in the changelog and the API reference. The old name would return a Deprecation header and a Sunset date. I would instrument requests so I can see which API keys still read the old field, and I would contact the two downstream owners directly with the timeline and a migration example. The old field is removed only after its usage has been at or near zero for an agreed period, such as two release cycles, and the owners have confirmed their cutover.\n\nThe ranking score change is more dangerous because the name stays the same while the meaning shifts, so silent changes would break clients without any error. I would not change the meaning of the existing field. Instead I would add a new field, such as `rank_score_v2`, with its own documented definition, and optionally let the third client opt in via a request parameter or API version header. I would run both scores in shadow mode, compare rank correlation and top-k overlap on sampled production queries, and only then decide on a default change. The old field keeps its original semantics until the same expand-and-contract process retires it. Throughout, I would track error rates, client-reported issues, and per-key adoption, and I would treat any unexpected drop in those signals as a reason to pause the timeline."},{"kind":"code","answer":"function solve(input) {\n  const items = JSON.parse(input);\n  const ids = items\n    .filter(q =>\n      Array.isArray(q.tags) &&\n      q.tags.includes(\"public\") &&\n      q.tags.includes(\"indexed\")\n    )\n    .map(q => q.id);\n  return JSON.stringify(ids);\n}"}],"_tests":{"passed":3,"total":3,"results":[{"input":"[{\"id\":\"a\",\"tags\":[\"public\",\"indexed\"]},{\"id\":\"b\",\"tags\":[\"public\"]},{\"id\":\"c\",\"tags\":[\"indexed\",\"public\",\"x\"]}]","expected":"[\"a\",\"c\"]","got":"[\"a\",\"c\"]","pass":true},{"input":"[]","expected":"[]","got":"[]","pass":true},{"input":"[{\"id\":\"z\",\"tags\":[\"private\"]}]","expected":"[]","got":"[]","pass":true}]},"grade":{"total":0.933,"parts":[{"kind":"short","score":0.9,"confidence":0.8,"note":"Gives a concrete request/response shape with limit cap, opaque cursor instead of offset, explicit filter allowlist with 400 errors on unknown fields, and additive-only schema evolution with schema_version, covering the rubric points."},{"kind":"scenario","score":0.9,"confidence":0.8,"note":"Uses expand-and-contract for the rename, adds a new score field instead of changing semantics, signals deprecation via headers and Sunset date, tracks per-key usage, and removes only after usage reaches zero or an agreed cutoff."},{"kind":"code","score":1,"confidence":0.95,"note":"Correct JSON.parse, filter requiring both tags, map to ids, JSON.stringify output preserving order; passes all 3 hidden tests and handles the empty case.","testsPassed":3,"testsTotal":3}],"regraded":false},"final":0.8287},"c049":{"id":"c049","name":"Aditya Novak","semantic":0.753704667091455,"seen":1,"coverage":{"m1":0.690389903857643,"m2":0.6522427497232146,"m3":0.6722444561973764,"n1":0.6795997463657473,"n2":0.6471191038994872},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":533,"end":629,"quote":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m2","start":313,"end":426,"quote":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m3","start":427,"end":532,"quote":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.93},{"requirementId":"n1","start":630,"end":731,"quote":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.9},{"requirementId":"n2","start":313,"end":426,"quote":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","strength":"partial","by":"claude","confidence":0.88}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_vzoa7p_c049","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","targets":["API design"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.","rubric":"A strong answer keeps the old field alongside the new one during a deprecation window, adds the new score under a new name rather than changing the meaning of the existing field, and uses versioning or response headers to signal deprecation. It also mentions tracking usage of the old field per client, notifying owners with a dated timeline, and removing the old behavior only after usage drops to zero or an agreed cutoff.","targets":["API design"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","rubric":"A good answer parses the input with JSON.parse, filters the array by checking that tags includes both required strings, maps to ids, and returns JSON.stringify of the result. It handles the empty case by returning \"[]\" and does not reorder elements.","targets":["API design"],"tests":[{"input":"[{\"id\":\"a\",\"tags\":[\"public\",\"indexed\"]},{\"id\":\"b\",\"tags\":[\"public\"]},{\"id\":\"c\",\"tags\":[\"indexed\",\"public\",\"x\"]}]","expected":"[\"a\",\"c\"]"},{"input":"[]","expected":"[]"},{"input":"[{\"id\":\"z\",\"tags\":[\"private\"]}]","expected":"[]"}]}],"emailSubject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","emailBody":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team","unverified":["API design"]},"sent":{"channel":"outbox","at":1793426298386},"responses":[{"kind":"short","answer":"I'd use a POST or GET search request with a query string, a `limit` (default 20, max 100) and an opaque `cursor` for pagination, plus a `filters` object whose keys are allow-listed and typed. The response would return `items` with `id`, `score`, and `metadata`, plus `next_cursor` and a `schema_version` field. Cursors would encode the last sort key rather than an offset, so inserts don't shift pages. For filter validation I'd validate against a versioned schema at the edge and reject unknown keys with a 400 that names the offending field, so new fields can be added without older clients silently changing results."},{"kind":"scenario","answer":"I'd treat both changes as additive first. For the rename, I'd return the new field name alongside the old one for at least two release cycles, mark the old one as deprecated in the OpenAPI spec, and log which clients still read it using request metadata or an API key tag. For the new ranking score, I wouldn't reuse the existing field name. I'd add a new field such as `score_v2` and keep the old field populated with its original meaning, and let the third client opt in through a request header or a `ranking_version` parameter. I'd announce a timeline by email and changelog with a concrete removal date, roughly 90 days out. The old behavior is safe to remove once access logs show zero calls to the deprecated field for two consecutive weeks and each known client has confirmed the migration in writing. I'd also keep a canary comparing old and new results on a labelled query set to catch regressions before the switch."},{"kind":"code","answer":"function solve(input) {\n  const queries = JSON.parse(input);\n  const ids = queries\n    .filter(q => Array.isArray(q.tags) && (q.tags.includes(\"public\") || q.tags.includes(\"indexed\")))\n    .map(q => q.id);\n  return JSON.stringify(ids);\n}"}],"_tests":{"passed":2,"total":3,"results":[{"input":"[{\"id\":\"a\",\"tags\":[\"public\",\"indexed\"]},{\"id\":\"b\",\"tags\":[\"public\"]},{\"id\":\"c\",\"tags\":[\"indexed\",\"public\",\"x\"]}]","expected":"[\"a\",\"c\"]","got":"[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"]","pass":false},{"input":"[]","expected":"[]","got":"[]","pass":true},{"input":"[{\"id\":\"z\",\"tags\":[\"private\"]}]","expected":"[]","got":"[]","pass":true}]},"grade":{"total":0.683,"parts":[{"kind":"short","score":0.85,"confidence":0.8,"note":"Gives a concrete request/response shape with limit, opaque cursor keyed on sort position, allow-listed typed filters returning 400 on unknown fields, and schema_version for stability; versioning is only lightly developed."},{"kind":"scenario","score":0.9,"confidence":0.85,"note":"Keeps the old field during a deprecation window, adds the new score under a new name, tracks per-client usage, sets a dated timeline, and removes only after zero usage plus written confirmation."},{"kind":"code","score":0.3,"confidence":0.9,"note":"Uses `||` instead of `&&`, so it returns ids with either tag rather than both; this matches the 2/3 hidden-test result, though JSON parsing, mapping, and the empty case are correct.","testsPassed":2,"testsTotal":3}],"regraded":false},"final":0.7254},"c066":{"id":"c066","name":"Elena Bose","semantic":0.733241025628987,"seen":1,"coverage":{"m1":0.6793442549338865,"m2":0.6284791737793802,"m3":0.6318097779492999,"n1":0.6795997463657473,"n2":0.6185761627349158},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":403,"end":499,"quote":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m2","start":590,"end":691,"quote":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.92},{"requirementId":"m3","start":297,"end":402,"quote":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.93},{"requirementId":"n1","start":590,"end":691,"quote":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.9},{"requirementId":"n2","start":500,"end":589,"quote":"Designed REST contracts for a dense passage ranking service used by three internal teams.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.93}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_vzoa7p_c066","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","targets":["API design"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.","rubric":"A strong answer keeps the old field alongside the new one during a deprecation window, adds the new score under a new name rather than changing the meaning of the existing field, and uses versioning or response headers to signal deprecation. It also mentions tracking usage of the old field per client, notifying owners with a dated timeline, and removing the old behavior only after usage drops to zero or an agreed cutoff.","targets":["API design"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","rubric":"A good answer parses the input with JSON.parse, filters the array by checking that tags includes both required strings, maps to ids, and returns JSON.stringify of the result. It handles the empty case by returning \"[]\" and does not reorder elements.","targets":["API design"],"tests":[{"input":"[{\"id\":\"a\",\"tags\":[\"public\",\"indexed\"]},{\"id\":\"b\",\"tags\":[\"public\"]},{\"id\":\"c\",\"tags\":[\"indexed\",\"public\",\"x\"]}]","expected":"[\"a\",\"c\"]"},{"input":"[]","expected":"[]"},{"input":"[{\"id\":\"z\",\"tags\":[\"private\"]}]","expected":"[]"}]}],"emailSubject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","emailBody":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team","unverified":["API design"]},"sent":{"channel":"outbox","at":1793426298386},"responses":[{"kind":"short","answer":"I would expose a GET /v1/search endpoint that takes a query string or vector reference, a filters object keyed by declared metadata fields, and a cursor plus limit for pagination. The response would return a results array of {id, score, metadata subset} plus a next_cursor that is opaque to clients, so I can change the underlying offset or keyset strategy later without breaking them. For pagination I would use keyset cursors over (score, id) rather than offsets, since offsets drift when the index is updated and get expensive at depth. For filters I would validate against a versioned schema at the gateway: unknown filter keys return a 400 with the list of supported fields, types and ranges are checked, and filters are normalized before they reach the index. To keep the API stable as the schema evolves, I would only add optional fields and filters, never change the meaning of existing ones, and document unknown-field handling as ignore-on-input and omit-on-output for clients that do not request them."},{"kind":"scenario","answer":"I would split this into two independent rollouts, each using expand-and-contract. For the field rename, I would start returning both the old and new field names in every response, mark the old name deprecated in the contract and changelog, and give clients a fixed date, typically 90 days out, before the old name is removed. I would message the two affected clients directly with the new name, a migration snippet, and a staging environment where they can verify. For the changed ranking score, I should not overload the existing field, because changing its meaning silently breaks anyone who interprets it. Instead I would add a new field such as rank_score_v2 with a documented definition, keep the old field computed as before for the duration of the window, and let the third client opt in via a request parameter or API version header. To know when it is safe to remove the old behavior, I would instrument per-client usage of the deprecated field and the old score path, watch them reach zero for at least two full weeks including any batch or monthly jobs, confirm with each client owner in writing, and only then remove the old behavior behind a flag first so that rollback is one switch away."},{"kind":"code","answer":"function solve(input) {\n  const items = JSON.parse(input);\n  const ids = items\n    .filter(q => Array.isArray(q.tags) && q.tags.includes(\"public\") && q.tags.includes(\"indexed\"))\n    .map(q => q.id);\n  return JSON.stringify(ids);\n}"}],"_tests":{"passed":3,"total":3,"results":[{"input":"[{\"id\":\"a\",\"tags\":[\"public\",\"indexed\"]},{\"id\":\"b\",\"tags\":[\"public\"]},{\"id\":\"c\",\"tags\":[\"indexed\",\"public\",\"x\"]}]","expected":"[\"a\",\"c\"]","got":"[\"a\",\"c\"]","pass":true},{"input":"[]","expected":"[]","got":"[]","pass":true},{"input":"[{\"id\":\"z\",\"tags\":[\"private\"]}]","expected":"[]","got":"[]","pass":true}]},"grade":{"total":0.933,"parts":[{"kind":"short","score":0.9,"confidence":0.85,"note":"Gives a concrete GET request with query, filters, cursor and limit, uses opaque keyset cursors instead of offsets, validates filters against declared fields with 400 errors, and keeps the API stable through additive-only changes."},{"kind":"scenario","score":0.9,"confidence":0.85,"note":"Uses expand-and-contract for the rename and adds a new score field instead of changing the old one's meaning, with dated deprecation, per-client usage tracking, owner notification, and removal only after usage reaches zero."},{"kind":"code","score":1,"confidence":0.95,"note":"Correctly parses, filters for both required tags, preserves order, and returns JSON.stringify output; passes all 3 hidden tests.","testsPassed":3,"testsTotal":3}],"regraded":false},"final":0.8131},"c002":{"id":"c002","name":"Hana Tanaka","semantic":0.726652351015367,"seen":1,"coverage":{"m1":0.6518026341364574,"m2":0.6360372742149399,"m3":0.6113217989083971,"n1":0.6795997463657473,"n2":0.6239845185420485},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":235,"end":331,"quote":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m2","start":540,"end":653,"quote":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m3","start":434,"end":539,"quote":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.93},{"requirementId":"n1","start":332,"end":433,"quote":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.9},{"requirementId":"n2","start":540,"end":653,"quote":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","strength":"partial","by":"claude","confidence":0.88}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_vzoa7p_c002","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","targets":["API design"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.","rubric":"A strong answer keeps the old field alongside the new one during a deprecation window, adds the new score under a new name rather than changing the meaning of the existing field, and uses versioning or response headers to signal deprecation. It also mentions tracking usage of the old field per client, notifying owners with a dated timeline, and removing the old behavior only after usage drops to zero or an agreed cutoff.","targets":["API design"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","rubric":"A good answer parses the input with JSON.parse, filters the array by checking that tags includes both required strings, maps to ids, and returns JSON.stringify of the result. It handles the empty case by returning \"[]\" and does not reorder elements.","targets":["API design"],"tests":[{"input":"[{\"id\":\"a\",\"tags\":[\"public\",\"indexed\"]},{\"id\":\"b\",\"tags\":[\"public\"]},{\"id\":\"c\",\"tags\":[\"indexed\",\"public\",\"x\"]}]","expected":"[\"a\",\"c\"]"},{"input":"[]","expected":"[]"},{"input":"[{\"id\":\"z\",\"tags\":[\"private\"]}]","expected":"[]"}]}],"emailSubject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","emailBody":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team","unverified":["API design"]},"sent":{"channel":"outbox","at":1793426298386},"responses":[{"kind":"short","answer":"I would take a page size and an opaque cursor as query params, and return a list of results with a next_cursor field. Filters would be passed as a small set of named metadata fields, validated with a Pydantic model so unknown fields get rejected. I would keep the response envelope simple so new fields can be added later without breaking clients."},{"kind":"scenario","answer":"I would add the new field alongside the old one and keep both populated for a while. For the ranking score, I would give it a new field name so the old field keeps its meaning. I would tell the clients about the change in the changelog and in a message, and set a date for removal. I would watch the request logs to see whether anyone still reads the old field, and remove it once usage drops to zero or the date passes."},{"kind":"code","answer":"function solve(input) {\n  const items = JSON.parse(input);\n  const out = [];\n  for (const q of items) {\n    if (q.tags.includes(\"public\")) {\n      out.push(q.id);\n    }\n  }\n  return JSON.stringify(out);\n}"}],"_tests":{"passed":2,"total":3,"results":[{"input":"[{\"id\":\"a\",\"tags\":[\"public\",\"indexed\"]},{\"id\":\"b\",\"tags\":[\"public\"]},{\"id\":\"c\",\"tags\":[\"indexed\",\"public\",\"x\"]}]","expected":"[\"a\",\"c\"]","got":"[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"]","pass":false},{"input":"[]","expected":"[]","got":"[]","pass":true},{"input":"[{\"id\":\"z\",\"tags\":[\"private\"]}]","expected":"[]","got":"[]","pass":true}]},"grade":{"total":0.45,"parts":[{"kind":"short","score":0.5,"confidence":0.7,"note":"Names a page size and opaque cursor with next_cursor and an extra-forbid filter model, but gives no concrete filter or result shape, no explicit 4xx error behavior, and no versioning strategy."},{"kind":"scenario","score":0.65,"confidence":0.7,"note":"Keeps the old field alongside the new one, gives the new score a new name, and ties removal to usage dropping to zero or a dated cutoff. It omits deprecation headers or versioning and only loosely covers per-client usage tracking."},{"kind":"code","score":0.2,"confidence":0.9,"note":"The filter checks only for \"public\" and never requires \"indexed\", so it returns wrong results; the hidden tests passing only 2 of 3 is consistent with this bug.","testsPassed":2,"testsTotal":3}],"regraded":false},"final":0.616},"c122":{"id":"c122","name":"Yuki Haddad","semantic":0.72262715292699,"seen":1,"coverage":{"m1":0.645019715101355,"m2":0.6516585494256081,"m3":0.5901246424509697,"n1":0.6795997463657473,"n2":0.6471191038994872},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":578,"end":674,"quote":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m2","start":272,"end":385,"quote":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m3","start":207,"end":230,"quote":"PostgreSQL query tuning","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.9},{"requirementId":"n1","start":476,"end":577,"quote":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.9},{"requirementId":"n2","start":386,"end":475,"quote":"Designed REST contracts for a dense passage ranking service used by three internal teams.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.93}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"final":0.7226},"c127":{"id":"c127","name":"Pooja Desai","semantic":0.705561637553545,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c178":{"id":"c178","name":"Neha Okafor","semantic":0.70194532894441,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c130":{"id":"c130","name":"Nora Mukherjee","semantic":0.688874721527178,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c147":{"id":"c147","name":"Ivan Desai","semantic":0.661349014928302,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c081":{"id":"c081","name":"Ines Nair","semantic":0.6600468146885,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c160":{"id":"c160","name":"Arjun Joshi","semantic":0.630107255007584,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_007006":{"id":"R_007006","name":"Hana Haddad","semantic":0.626912818043052,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]}},"shortlist":["c156","c049","c066","c002","c122"],"memoryApplied":["Senior Backend Engineer, Retrieval Platform. Must-haves: vector search, Python, Postgres. Nice: embeddings pipeline, API design. The pool's strongest matches were pgvector/FAISS/HNSW + FastAPI profiles, found with a pgvector-focused query. Postgres evidence is often a strengths-list mention, so cite project sentences such as the pgvector/HNSW migration. Five candidates were fully evidenced and approved (c156, c122, c049, c066, c002). Assessment on API design and embeddings gave grades 0.93, 0.93, 0.68, 0.45, 0.28; the code tests were the main differentiator. c122 had only partial Postgres evidence and the lowest grade. The JD co