SQL and modelling data warehouse tables for analytics","view":"visible","k":12,"source":"pgvector","top":[{"id":"R_006660","score":0.768},{"id":"R_006307","score":0.762},{"id":"R_003131","score":0.758},{"id":"R_003760","score":0.758},{"id":"R_005513","score":0.758},{"id":"R_007123","score":0.756},{"id":"R_001472","score":0.755},{"id":"R_009408","score":0.755},{"id":"R_001950","score":0.753},{"id":"R_007800","score":0.752}]}],"candidates":{"c030":{"id":"c030","name":"Sam Nair","semantic":0.785147811752824,"seen":1,"coverage":{"m1":0.6393052755572268,"m2":0.6466165548812286,"m3":0.6425400326449446,"n1":0.4901546783880886,"n2":0.6344562202670962},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":391,"end":486,"quote":"Rebuilt nightly PySpark jobs on Databricks and cut the batch window from 6 hours to 50 minutes.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m2","start":289,"end":390,"quote":"Wrote the SQL that reconciles finance and product marts, with window functions and tested dbt models.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.96},{"requirementId":"m3","start":391,"end":486,"quote":"Rebuilt nightly PySpark jobs on Databricks and cut the batch window from 6 hours to 50 minutes.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.95},{"requirementId":"n1","start":243,"end":260,"quote":"DAG orchestration","strength":"partial","by":"claude","confidence":0.7},{"requirementId":"n2","start":554,"end":633,"quote":"Wrote Kafka consumers that land clickstream events in BigQuery within a minute.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.95}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_vzrdvj_c030","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"In Airflow, what is the difference between a DAG and a DAG run, and what is a task instance? Then explain why tasks should be idempotent when Airflow retries them.","rubric":"A good answer says a DAG is the definition of tasks and dependencies, a DAG run is one scheduled or triggered execution of that DAG for a logical date, and a task instance is one task within one DAG run. It explains that retries can re-execute a task, so repeated runs must produce the same result without duplicating data, for example by overwriting a partition or using upserts.","targets":["Airflow"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"You own a daily Airflow DAG that loads yesterday's orders into a warehouse table. The load task fails halfway through, after writing about 60% of the rows. The on-call engineer asks you how to rerun it safely. Describe what you would check first, how you would rerun the DAG, and what you would change so the next failure does not cause the same problem.","rubric":"A strong answer checks the partial write and the task state in the Airflow UI or logs before rerunning. It proposes clearing only the failed task or run, and making the load idempotent by deleting or overwriting the target partition for that logical date or using a staging table and an atomic swap. It also mentions setting sensible retries and alerting on failure.","targets":["Airflow"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Write a JavaScript function solve(input) that takes a string of dependency edges separated by spaces. Each edge looks like \"a>b\", meaning task a must run before task b. Return the task names in a valid run order as a comma-separated string with no spaces. When several tasks are ready at the same time, pick the alphabetically smallest first. If the edges contain a cycle, return \"cycle\". Only tasks that appear in an edge are included. An empty input returns an empty string.","rubric":"A correct solution builds a directed graph and in-degree counts from the edges, then runs Kahn's algorithm with a sorted ready set so ties go to the alphabetically smallest task. It returns \"cycle\" when not all nodes are emitted. Accept any correct topological method that respects the tie-break rule.","targets":["Airflow"],"tests":[{"input":"extract>transform transform>load","expected":"extract,transform,load"},{"input":"b>d a>d c>a","expected":"b,c,a,d"},{"input":"a>b b>a","expected":"cycle"}]}],"emailSubject":"Data Engineer (mid) assessment: three tasks, about 45 minutes","emailBody":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Data Engineer (mid) role. We would like you to complete a short assessment before we talk further.\n\nIt has three tasks. The first is a short written question about Airflow. The second is a short scenario about rerunning a failed pipeline step. The third is a small JavaScript exercise that you write as a single function called solve.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes. You do not need to finish everything perfectly. We want to see how you reason.\n\nReply to this email with your answers when you are done. If anything in the instructions is unclear, ask us before you start.\n\nThe hiring team","unverified":["Airflow"]},"sent":{"channel":"outbox","at":1793426472908},"responses":[{"kind":"short","answer":"A DAG is the definition of a workflow: a directed acyclic graph of tasks and their dependencies, defined once in code. A DAG run is one execution of that DAG for a particular logical date or data interval, such as the run for 2024-05-01. A task instance is one task inside one DAG run, with its own state (queued, running, success, failed, up_for_retry) and its own try count. Tasks should be idempotent because Airflow retries failed task instances automatically, and it can also rerun them with a manual clear. If a retry repeats the side effects of a partial earlier attempt, for example by appending rows that were already written, the result is duplicated or inconsistent data. An idempotent task produces the same end state no matter how many times it runs for the same logical date. In practice this means deleting or overwriting the target partition for that date, or using MERGE on a natural key, and writing through a staging table that is swapped in only after the load completes."},{"kind":"scenario","answer":"First I would not rerun anything yet. I would confirm the failure state: which task instance failed, the error in its log, and how many rows actually landed in the target table for yesterday's partition. I would compare that with the source row count for the same window. I would also check whether any downstream task has already run against the partial data, and whether anyone is querying the table right now.\n\nTo rerun safely, I would first make sure the load is idempotent for that date. If the task appends, I would delete or overwrite yesterday's partition in the target before reloading, not just retry the task. If the write goes through a staging table, I would clear the staging rows for that run and rebuild them. Then I would clear only the failed load task instance, not the whole DAG, so upstream extracts are not repeated unless they are also stale. After it succeeds, I would check the row count against the source, check key uniqueness, and then clear or rerun the downstream tasks that depend on the table.\n\nTo prevent the same problem next time, I would change the write pattern so a partial failure cannot leave a visible half-load. Options include writing to a staging table and swapping partitions or tables atomically on success, using MERGE keyed on order_id so retries are harmless, and wrapping the load in a single transaction where the warehouse supports it. I would add a post-load validation task that compares the loaded count to the source and fails the DAG if they differ, so the table is never marked complete with missing rows. I would also set sensible retries with a backoff, and alert on-call when a load fails partway instead of relying on someone to notice the gap."},{"kind":"code","answer":"function solve(input) {\n  const tokens = String(input == null ? '' : input).trim().split(/\\s+/).filter(function (t) { return t.length > 0; });\n  if (tokens.length === 0) return '';\n\n  const nodes = new Set();\n  const adj = new Map();\n  const indeg = new Map();\n  const seenEdges = new Set();\n\n  for (const tok of tokens) {\n    const parts = tok.split('>');\n    if (parts.length !== 2 || parts[0] === '' || parts[1] === '') continue;\n    const a = parts[0];\n    const b = parts[1];\n    const key = a + '>' + b;\n    if (seenEdges.has(key)) continue;\n    seenEdges.add(key);\n\n    nodes.add(a);\n    nodes.add(b);\n    if (!adj.has(a)) adj.set(a, []);\n    adj.get(a).push(b);\n    if (!indeg.has(a)) indeg.set(a, 0);\n    if (!indeg.has(b)) indeg.set(b, 0);\n    indeg.set(b, indeg.get(b) + 1);\n  }\n\n  const done = new Set();\n  const order = [];\n  while (order.length < nodes.size) {\n    const ready = [];\n    for (const n of nodes) {\n      if (!done.has(n) && indeg.get(n) === 0) ready.push(n);\n    }\n    if (ready.length === 0) return 'cycle';\n    ready.sort();\n    const n = ready[0];\n    done.add(n);\n    order.push(n);\n    for (const m of (adj.get(n) || [])) {\n      indeg.set(m, indeg.get(m) - 1);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return order.join(',');\n}"}],"final":0.7851},"c051":{"id":"c051","name":"Manish Petrov","semantic":0.748539395499774,"seen":1,"coverage":{"m1":0.6511999537544182,"m2":0.651556083554375,"m3":0.6475363034657154,"n1":0.4917691700674964,"n2":0.5416313859744387},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":335,"end":430,"quote":"Rebuilt nightly PySpark jobs on Databricks and cut the batch window from 6 hours to 50 minutes.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m2","start":498,"end":599,"quote":"Wrote the SQL that reconciles finance and product marts, with window functions and tested dbt models.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.96},{"requirementId":"m3","start":261,"end":334,"quote":"Owned DAG orchestration for 200+ daily tasks with retries and SLA alerts.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.95},{"requirementId":"n1","start":261,"end":334,"quote":"Owned DAG orchestration for 200+ daily tasks with retries and SLA alerts.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.95}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_vzrdvj_c051","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"In two or three sentences, explain the difference between event time and processing time in a streaming pipeline, and give one concrete case where using the wrong one would produce incorrect aggregates.","rubric":"A good answer says event time is when the event happened at its source and processing time is when the system sees it. It gives a concrete example such as late or out-of-order mobile events landing in the wrong hourly window when bucketed by arrival time. Watermarks or allowed lateness as a way to handle this earn extra credit but are not required.","targets":["Streaming"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"You own a pipeline that reads clickstream events from a Kafka topic and writes hourly page-view counts per country to a warehouse table used by a daily dashboard. Some events arrive up to 30 minutes late, and occasionally the job restarts after a failure. Describe how you would design the streaming job so that the counts are correct and the restarts do not create duplicates. Name the specific mechanisms you would use and explain what you would check after a restart.","rubric":"A strong answer uses event-time windowing with a watermark sized to the 30-minute lateness, and mentions checkpointing of offsets and state so the job resumes from a known point. It explains that duplicates are avoided through idempotent or transactional writes, such as merge or upsert keyed on window and country, or exactly-once sinks. It also names concrete post-restart checks, such as comparing row counts or sums against the source for a recent window.","targets":["Streaming","Data pipelines"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Implement a function solve(input) that takes a string of newline-separated event records in the form 'timestamp_seconds,key,value' and returns a string. Records may arrive out of order. For each key, compute the sum of values inside tumbling 60-second windows, where a window starts at floor(timestamp/60)*60. Output one line per (window_start, key) pair, sorted by window_start ascending and then by key ascending, formatted as 'window_start,key,sum'. Ignore blank lines. Return the lines joined with newlines, with no trailing newline.","rubric":"A correct solution parses each non-blank line, buckets by floor division of the timestamp by 60 times 60, accumulates sums in a map keyed by window and key, then sorts by window then key before formatting. It should not depend on input order. Handling of blank lines and the absence of a trailing newline are the most common points of failure.","targets":["Streaming"],"tests":[{"input":"120,a,5\n10,a,2\n70,b,3\n65,a,1\n","expected":"0,a,2\n60,a,1\n60,b,3\n120,a,5"},{"input":"0,x,1\n\n59,x,2\n60,x,4\n","expected":"0,x,3\n60,x,4"},{"input":"300,z,10\n299,z,1\n","expected":"240,z,1\n300,z,10"}]}],"emailSubject":"Data Engineer (mid) take-home assessment","emailBody":"Hi,\n\nThanks for applying for the Data Engineer (mid) role. We would like you to complete a short assessment so we can look more closely at your streaming work, which we could not confirm from your profile.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. The first is one short written answer about streaming concepts. The second is a scenario about designing a streaming job for late and duplicate events. The third is a small JavaScript function with three test cases.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes. Work on it alone and send back your answers and the code when you are done.\n\nThe hiring team","unverified":["Streaming"]},"sent":{"channel":"outbox","at":1793426472908},"responses":[{"kind":"short","answer":"Event time is the timestamp recorded when the event actually happened at its source, while processing time is when the pipeline receives or handles it. Windowed aggregates should be keyed on event time so late or out-of-order arrivals land in the correct window. For example, if a purchase made at 10:59:50 arrives at 11:00:20 and the job buckets by processing time, that sale is counted in the 11:00 hour, which understates 10:00 and overstates 11:00."},{"kind":"scenario","answer":"I would read from Kafka with Structured Streaming and parse the event timestamp from the payload, then set a watermark of 30 minutes on that event-time column so state for each hourly window is kept until events up to 30 minutes late have arrived. I would aggregate with a tumbling window of one hour grouped by country, which gives page-view counts per window and country. For idempotent output I would write each micro-batch to the warehouse with foreach-batch and a MERGE keyed on (window_start, country), so a replayed batch overwrites rather than appends. Checkpointing to durable storage tracks Kafka offsets and state, so a restart resumes from the last committed batch instead of reprocessing from the beginning. After a restart I would verify that the checkpoint offsets advanced monotonically, that the row count per window matches a batch recount from the raw topic for a sample of recent hours, that no (window_start, country) key appears more than once in the table, and that the late-data drop metric is consistent with the expected 30-minute tail. I would also confirm that the daily dashboard totals reconcile with the sum of the hourly rows."},{"kind":"code","answer":"function solve(input) {\n  const byWindow = new Map();\n  for (const raw of input.split('\\n')) {\n    const line = raw.trim();\n    if (line === '') continue;\n    const parts = line.split(',');\n    const ts = Number(parts[0]);\n    const key = parts[1];\n    const value = Number(parts[2]);\n    const windowStart = Math.floor(ts / 60) * 60;\n    if (!byWindow.has(windowStart)) byWindow.set(windowStart, new Map());\n    const keys = byWindow.get(windowStart);\n    keys.set(key, (keys.get(key) || 0) + value);\n  }\n  const windows = [...byWindow.keys()].sort((a, b) => a - b);\n  const lines = [];\n  for (const w of windows) {\n    const keys = [...byWindow.get(w).keys()].sort((a, b) => (a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0));\n    for (const k of keys) {\n      lines.push(`${w},${k},${byWindow.get(w).get(k)}`);\n    }\n  }\n  return lines.join('\\n');\n}"}],"final":0.7485},"c177":{"id":"c177","name":"Jay Moreau","semantic":0.731461997380754,"seen":1,"coverage":{"m1":0.7650434654237276,"m2":0.6420727719850159,"m3":0.8687771221827613,"n1":0.6210056027688468,"n2":0.6757304324436046},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c041":{"id":"c041","name":"Aditya Fischer","semantic":0.725018122785809,"seen":1,"coverage":{"m1":0.6235019919599807,"m2":0.5986161527244346,"m3":0.6212814054269947,"n1":0.5055857384695848,"n2":0.6344562202670962},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":300,"end":395,"quote":"Rebuilt nightly PySpark jobs on Databricks and cut the batch window from 6 hours to 50 minutes.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m2","start":476,"end":542,"quote":"Modelled a Snowflake warehouse with dbt, with tests on every mart.","strength":"partial","by":"claude","confidence":0.85},{"requirementId":"m3","start":543,"end":616,"quote":"Owned DAG orchestration for 200+ daily tasks with retries and SLA alerts.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.95},{"requirementId":"n1","start":543,"end":616,"quote":"Owned DAG orchestration for 200+ daily tasks with retries and SLA alerts.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.95},{"requirementId":"n2","start":396,"end":475,"quote":"Wrote Kafka consumers that land clickstream events in BigQuery within a minute.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.95}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"final":0.725},"R_005044":{"id":"R_005044","name":"Dev Garcia","semantic":0.718528006252293,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c171":{"id":"c171","name":"Elias Nair","semantic":0.713676691055298,"seen":1,"coverage":{"m1":0.6235019919599807,"m2":0.651556083554375,"m3":0.619071436352712,"n1":0.5055857384695848,"n2":0.5824746038714795},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":242,"end":337,"quote":"Rebuilt nightly PySpark jobs on Databricks and cut the batch window from 6 hours to 50 minutes.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m2","start":405,"end":506,"quote":"Wrote the SQL that reconciles finance and product marts, with window functions and tested dbt models.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.96},{"requirementId":"m3","start":507,"end":580,"quote":"Owned DAG orchestration for 200+ daily tasks with retries and SLA alerts.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.95},{"requirementId":"n1","start":507,"end":580,"quote":"Owned DAG orchestration for 200+ daily tasks with retries and SLA alerts.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.95}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_vzrdvj_c171","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"In two or three sentences, explain the difference between event time and processing time in a streaming pipeline, and give one concrete case where using the wrong one would produce incorrect aggregates.","rubric":"A good answer says event time is when the event happened at its source and processing time is when the system sees it. It gives a concrete example such as late or out-of-order mobile events landing in the wrong hourly window when bucketed by arrival time. Watermarks or allowed lateness as a way to handle this earn extra credit but are not required.","targets":["Streaming"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"You own a pipeline that reads clickstream events from a Kafka topic and writes hourly page-view counts per country to a warehouse table used by a daily dashboard. Some events arrive up to 30 minutes late, and occasionally the job restarts after a failure. Describe how you would design the streaming job so that the counts are correct and the restarts do not create duplicates. Name the specific mechanisms you would use and explain what you would check after a restart.","rubric":"A strong answer uses event-time windowing with a watermark sized to the 30-minute lateness, and mentions checkpointing of offsets and state so the job resumes from a known point. It explains that duplicates are avoided through idempotent or transactional writes, such as merge or upsert keyed on window and country, or exactly-once sinks. It also names concrete post-restart checks, such as comparing row counts or sums against the source for a recent window.","targets":["Streaming","Data pipelines"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Implement a function solve(input) that takes a string of newline-separated event records in the form 'timestamp_seconds,key,value' and returns a string. Records may arrive out of order. For each key, compute the sum of values inside tumbling 60-second windows, where a window starts at floor(timestamp/60)*60. Output one line per (window_start, key) pair, sorted by window_start ascending and then by key ascending, formatted as 'window_start,key,sum'. Ignore blank lines. Return the lines joined with newlines, with no trailing newline.","rubric":"A correct solution parses each non-blank line, buckets by floor division of the timestamp by 60 times 60, accumulates sums in a map keyed by window and key, then sorts by window then key before formatting. It should not depend on input order. Handling of blank lines and the absence of a trailing newline are the most common points of failure.","targets":["Streaming"],"tests":[{"input":"120,a,5\n10,a,2\n70,b,3\n65,a,1\n","expected":"0,a,2\n60,a,1\n60,b,3\n120,a,5"},{"input":"0,x,1\n\n59,x,2\n60,x,4\n","expected":"0,x,3\n60,x,4"},{"input":"300,z,10\n299,z,1\n","expected":"240,z,1\n300,z,10"}]}],"emailSubject":"Data Engineer (mid) take-home assessment","emailBody":"Hi,\n\nThanks for applying for the Data Engineer (mid) role. We would like you to complete a short assessment so we can look more closely at your streaming work, which we could not confirm from your profile.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. The first is one short written answer about streaming concepts. The second is a scenario about designing a streaming job for late and duplicate events. The third is a small JavaScript function with three test cases.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes. Work on it alone and send back your answers and the code when you are done.\n\nThe hiring team","unverified":["Streaming"]},"sent":{"channel":"outbox","at":1793426472908},"responses":[{"kind":"short","answer":"Event time is the timestamp recorded when the event actually happened at the source, while processing time is the clock time when the pipeline sees the event. Aggregates should be bucketed by event time so that late or out-of-order events land in the correct window. For example, if a mobile app batches clicks made at 10:59 and uploads them at 11:03, counting by processing time puts those clicks in the 11:00 hour, which inflates that hour and undercounts 10:00."},{"kind":"scenario","answer":"I would use Spark Structured Streaming (or the equivalent in the team's engine) reading from Kafka with the event timestamp taken from the payload, not the broker or ingest time. I would set a watermark of 30 minutes on the event-time column so the window state can be finalised once the watermark passes the window end, and I would aggregate with a tumbling one-hour window grouped by country. Events older than the watermark are dropped by the engine, so I would also route them to a side output or a late-events table and reconcile that volume daily so the trade-off is visible.\n\nFor exactly-once output I would rely on the checkpoint location, which stores the Kafka offsets and the state store together, so a restart resumes from a consistent point. The sink would be idempotent: I would write using MERGE on (window_start, country) keyed upserts into the warehouse table, or use the foreachBatch pattern with a batch id recorded in a control table so a replayed micro-batch is skipped. Writing blind appends would create duplicates, so I would avoid that.\n\nAfter a restart I would check that the checkpoint offsets advanced monotonically, that the number of rows per window in the warehouse matches a recount from the raw topic for a sample of recent hours, that no batch id appears twice in the control table, and that the late-event count is in line with the historical baseline. I would also confirm the daily dashboard totals match the source for the previous day before the dashboard refreshes."},{"kind":"code","answer":"function solve(input) {\n  const buckets = new Map();\n  for (const raw of input.split('\\n')) {\n    const line = raw.trim();\n    if (line === '') continue;\n    const parts = line.split(',');\n    const ts = Number(parts[0]);\n    const key = parts[1];\n    const value = Number(parts[2]);\n    const windowStart = Math.floor(ts / 60) * 60;\n    const bucketKey = JSON.stringify([windowStart, key]);\n    buckets.set(bucketKey, (buckets.get(bucketKey) || 0) + value);\n  }\n  const rows = [];\n  for (const [bucketKey, sum] of buckets) {\n    const [windowStart, key] = JSON.parse(bucketKey);\n    rows.push({ windowStart, key, sum });\n  }\n  rows.sort((a, b) => {\n    if (a.windowStart !== b.windowStart) return a.windowStart - b.windowStart;\n    if (a.key < b.key) return -1;\n    if (a.key > b.key) return 1;\n    return 0;\n  });\n  return rows.map(r => `${r.windowStart},${r.key},${r.sum}`).join('\\n');\n}"}],"final":0.7137},"c118":{"id":"c118","name":"Pranav Saxena","semantic":0.699740844980429,"seen":1,"coverage":{"m1":0.6085744598536588,"m2":0.651556083554375,"m3":0.6300854018671573,"n1":0.4901546783880886,"n2":0.6577485309953508},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":520,"end":615,"quote":"Rebuilt nightly PySpark jobs on Databricks and cut the batch window from 6 hours to 50 minutes.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.97},{"requirementId":"m2","start":351,"end":452,"quote":"Wrote the SQL that reconciles finance and product marts, with window functions and tested dbt models.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.96},{"requirementId":"m3","start":271,"end":350,"quote":"Wrote Kafka consumers that land clickstream events in BigQuery within a minute.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.95},{"requirementId":"n2","start":271,"end":350,"quote":"Wrote Kafka consumers that land clickstream events in BigQuery within a minute.","strength":"strong","by":"claude","confidence":0.95}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_vzrdvj_c118","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"In Airflow, what is the difference between a DAG and a DAG run, and what is a task instance? Then explain why tasks should be idempotent when Airflow retries them.","rubric":"A good answer says a DAG is the definition of tasks and dependencies, a DAG run is one scheduled or triggered execution of that DAG for a logical date, and a task instance is one task within one DAG run. It explains that retries can re-execute a task, so repeated runs must produce the same result without duplicating data, for example by overwriting a partition or using upserts.","targets":["Airflow"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"You own a daily Airflow DAG that loads yesterday's orders into a warehouse table. The load task fails halfway through, after writing about 60% of the rows. The on-call engineer asks you how to rerun it safely. Describe what you would check first, how you would rerun the DAG, and what you would change so the next failure does not cause the same problem.","rubric":"A strong answer checks the partial write and the task state in the Airflow UI or logs before rerunning. It proposes clearing only the failed task or run, and making the load idempotent by deleting or overwriting the target partition for that logical date or using a staging table and an atomic swap. It also mentions setting sensible retries and alerting on failure.","targets":["Airflow"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Write a JavaScript function solve(input) that takes a string of dependency edges separated by spaces. Each edge looks like \"a>b\", meaning task a must run before task b. Return the task names in a valid run order as a comma-separated string with no spaces. When several tasks are ready at the same time, pick the alphabetically smallest first. If the edges contain a cycle, return \"cycle\". Only tasks that appear in an edge are included. An empty input returns an empty string.","rubric":"A correct solution builds a directed graph and in-degree counts from the edges, then runs Kahn's algorithm with a sorted ready set so ties go to the alphabetically smallest task. It returns \"cycle\" when not all nodes are emitted. Accept any correct topological method that respects the tie-break rule.","targets":["Airflow"],"tests":[{"input":"extract>transform transform>load","expected":"extract,transform,load"},{"input":"b>d a>d c>a","expected":"b,c,a,d"},{"input":"a>b b>a","expected":"cycle"}]}],"emailSubject":"Data Engineer (mid) assessment: three tasks, about 45 minutes","emailBody":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Data Engineer (mid) role. We would like you to complete a short assessment before we talk further.\n\nIt has three tasks. The first is a short written question about Airflow. The second is a short scenario about rerunning a failed pipeline step. The third is a small JavaScript exercise that you write as a single function called solve.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes. You do not need to finish everything perfectly. We want to see how you reason.\n\nReply to this email with your answers when you are done. If anything in the instructions is unclear, ask us before you start.\n\nThe hiring team","unverified":["Airflow"]},"sent":{"channel":"outbox","at":1793426472908},"responses":[{"kind":"short","answer":"A DAG is the definition of a workflow: the set of tasks and their dependency edges, with no execution attached. A DAG run is one execution of that DAG for a particular logical date or interval. A task instance is one task inside one DAG run, and it has its own state such as queued, running, failed, or success. Tasks should be idempotent because Airflow retries failed task instances automatically. If a retry repeats side effects, such as inserting the same rows twice, the result is duplicated or corrupted data. An idempotent task produces the same end state no matter how many times it runs, for example by overwriting a partition or using MERGE on a natural key."},{"kind":"scenario","answer":"First I would check the state of the target table and the task logs. I want to know how many rows were written, whether the load used a transaction, and whether any partial batch is visible to downstream readers. I would also confirm the source row count for yesterday's partition so I can compare it with what landed.\n\nTo rerun, I would clear the failed task instance in Airflow rather than trigger a whole new run, so the DAG run keeps its logical date. Before the rerun I would delete yesterday's partition or the rows for that date from the target, then let the load write them again. If the load is not partitioned by date, I would use a MERGE on order_id so existing rows get updated instead of duplicated.\n\nFor the future, I would make the load idempotent by writing per partition with overwrite or delete-then-insert inside one transaction. I would add a row-count check after the load that fails the task if the count does not match the source. I would also set retries with a short backoff and add an alert to the on-call channel for any load that writes fewer rows than expected."},{"kind":"code","answer":"function solve(input) {\n  if (!input || !input.trim()) return \"\";\n  const edges = input.trim().split(/\\s+/).map(e => e.split(\">\"));\n  const nodes = new Set();\n  const indeg = {};\n  const adj = {};\n  for (const [a, b] of edges) {\n    nodes.add(a);\n    nodes.add(b);\n    if (!adj[a]) adj[a] = [];\n    adj[a].push(b);\n    indeg[b] = (indeg[b] || 0) + 1;\n    if (indeg[a] === undefined) indeg[a] = 0;\n  }\n  const ready = [...nodes].filter(n => indeg[n] === 0);\n  const order = [];\n  while (ready.length) {\n    const n = ready.shift();\n    order.push(n);\n    for (const m of adj[n] || []) {\n      indeg[m]--;\n      if (indeg[m] === 0) ready.push(m);\n    }\n  }\n  if (order.length !== nodes.size) return \"cycle\";\n  return order.join(\",\");\n}"}],"final":0.6997},"R_006810":{"id":"R_006810","name":"Manish Joshi","semantic":0.69881135800089,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c016":{"id":"c016","name":"Samuel Pillai","semantic":0.69508478993091,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_001844":{"id":"R_001844","name":"Yusuf Joshi","semantic":0.694413459966967,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_001204":{"id":"R_001204","name":"Aditya Kulkarni","semantic":0.694150494134564,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_001479":{"id":"R_001479","name":"Yusuf Joshi","semantic":0.693992336267889,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_006660":{"id":"R_006660","name":"Neha Joshi","semantic":0.768014894009558,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_006307":{"id":"R_006307","name":"Dev Lindqvist","semantic":0.761694822917653,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_003131":{"id":"R_003131","name":"Rahul Ahmed","semantic":0.757909388293003,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_003760":{"id":"R_003760","name":"Vikram Fischer","semantic":0.757885127319684,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_005513":{"id":"R_005513","name":"Aditya Garcia","semantic":0.757504640220489,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_007123":{"id":"R_007123","name":"Manish Fischer","semantic":0.755976017015531,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_001472":{"id":"R_001472","name":"Manish Tanaka","semantic":0.754806444335401,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_009408":{"id":"R_009408","name":"Karthik Fischer","semantic":0.75452093806991,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_001950":{"id":"R_001950","name":"Nandini Joshi","semantic":0.752558528157004,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_007800":{"id":"R_007800","name":"Omar Fischer","semantic":0.752214162222098,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_009120":{"id":"R_009120","name":"Robin Fischer","semantic":0.752075016993374,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_001515":{"id":"R_001515","name":"Neha Pillai","semantic":0.751825503841751,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]}},"shortlist":["c030","c041","c051","c171","c118"],"memoryApplied":[],"degraded":[],"nudges":0,"approval":{"approved":["c030","c041","c051","c171","c118"],"removed":[]},"audit":{"ids":["c030","c041","c051","c171","c118"],"weights":{"semantic":0.6,"assessment":0.4},"rows":[{"id":"c030","name":"Sam Nair","gender":"non-binary","tier":3,"visibleRank":1,"hiddenRank":1,"delta":0,"visibleScore":0.6614,"hiddenScore":0.722},{"id":"c118","name":"Pranav Saxena","gender":"male","tier":3,"visibleRank":2,"hiddenRank":2,"delta":0,"visibleScore":0.6539,"hiddenScore":0.718},{"id":"c041","name":"Aditya Fischer","gender":"male","tier":3,"visibleRank":3,"hiddenRank":4,"delta":-1,"visibleScore":0.6347,"hiddenScore":0.6711},{"id":"c171","name":"Elias Nair","gender":"male","tier":1,"visibleRank":4,"hiddenRank":3,"delta":1,"visibleScore":0.6167,"hiddenScore":0.6717},{"id":"c051","name":"Manish Petrov","gender":"male","tier":2,"visibleRank":5,"hiddenRank":5,"delta":0,"visibleScore":0.6127,"hiddenScore":0.6646}],"spearman":0.9,"top5Overlap":1,"split":{"visible":{"gender":{"non-binary":1,"male":4},"tier":{"tier3":3,"tier1":1,"tier2":1}},"hidden":{"gender":{"non-binary":1,"male":4},"tier":{"tier3":3,"tier1":1,"tier2":1}}},"findings":["Top-5 overlap is 100% and Spearman ρ is 0.9: identity had little effect on this ranking."],"recommendBlind":false},"final":{"ranked":["c030","c051","c041","c171","c118"],"weights":{"semantic":1,"assessment":0},"summary":"Deterministic finish. 26 tool calls, 0 reformulations, 0 dropped (none). Fairness: rho 0.9, top-5 overlap 1.","officialBlind":false},"runSummary":{"toolCalls":27,"reformulations":0,"dropped":[],"llm":{"calls":70,"cached":26,"cost":0.07207,"budget":3,"totalSpent":0.53703,"remaining":2.46297},"cascade":{"decisions":33,"byTier":{"local":11,"jev":0,"claude":22},"resolvedBeforeClaude":0.333,"resolvedByJev":0,"escalationRate":0.667,"actualUsd":0.0009,"allClaudeUsd":0.00306}}},"summary":{"toolCalls":27,"reformulations":0,"dropped":[],"llm":{"calls":70,"cached":26,"cost":0.07207,"budget":3,"totalSpent":0.53703,"remaining":2.46297},"cascade":{"decisions":33,"byTier":{"local":11,"jev":0,"claude":22},"resolvedBeforeClaude":0.333,"resolvedByJev":0,"escalationRate":0.667,"actualUsd":0.0009,"allClaudeUsd":0.00306}}},"run_mvvzzku9":{"id":"run_mvvzzku9","roleKey":"custom","jdText":"Backend Engineer, Payments\n\nWe need a backend engineer for our payments team. Male candidates only. Must be comfortable with on-call.\n\nMust have: Java, PostgreSQL, distributed systems.\nNice to have: Kafka.\nSeniority: mid.","status":"running","chaos":{},"createdAt":1793426760513,"updatedAt":1793426760513},"run_mvw030g8":{"id":"run_mvw030g8","roleKey":"llm-apps","jdText":"LLM Application Engineer\n\nLLM application engineer: prompt engineering, RAG, agents, and evaluation.\n\nMust have: LLM applications, evaluation.\nNice to have: prompt engineering, RAG, agents.\nSeniority: mid.","presetId":"jd10","status":"done","chaos":{},"createdAt":1793426920712,"updatedAt":1793427026034,"state":{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","roleKey":"llm-apps","chaos":{},"jd":{"raw":"LLM Application Engineer\n\nLLM application engineer: prompt engineering, RAG, agents, and evaluation.\n\nMust have: LLM applications, evaluation.\nNice to have: prompt engineering, RAG, agents.\nSeniority: mid.","title":"LLM Application Engineer","seniority":"mid","requirements":[{"id":"m1","text":"LLM applications","kind":"must"},{"id":"m2","text":"evaluation","kind":"must"},{"id":"n1","text":"prompt engineering","kind":"nice"},{"id":"n2","text":"RAG","kind":"nice"},{"id":"n3","text":"agents","kind":"nice"}],"refused":[]},"phase":"DONE","targetN":5,"reformulations":1,"toolCalls":[{"n":1,"name":"recall_memory","reason":"Start of session; JD instructs checking saved HR notes for this role before searching.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"role\":\"llm-apps\",\"notes\":[],\"applied\":0}","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":2,"name":"search_candidates","reason":"No memory notes; first cluster is m1 LLM applications, searching semantically for engineers who built LLM-powered applications.","ok":true,"summary":"12 hits, top undefined","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":3,"name":"search_candidates","reason":"Second must-have cluster (m2 evaluation): the first search surfaced only two llm-apps profiles (c060, c131), so I need candidates who evaluate LLM outputs.","ok":true,"summary":"12 hits, top undefined","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":4,"name":"get_profile","reason":"c060 is the top llm-apps match from search 1 and is tagged with both must-haves; need to verify the claims.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"id\":\"c060\",\"name\":\"Ravi Sharma\",\"yoe\":0,\"skills\":[\"LLM applications\",\"prompt engineering\",\"RAG\",\"a","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":5,"name":"cite_evidence","reason":"c060 has keyword-level matches for m1 and m2 only; testing whether any sentence carries real substance before deciding to drop.","ok":true,"summary":"rejected: undefined","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":6,"name":"drop_candidate","reason":"c060 claims failed citation: m1 and m2 rest on keyword lists and a \"passionate about\" line, with no built work described.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"dropped\":\"c060\",\"remainingWithEvidence\":0}","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":7,"name":"get_profile","reason":"c131 is the second llm-apps profile from search 1 and the only remaining lead with an LLM-applications tag; checking whether it has concrete build or evaluation work.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"id\":\"c131\",\"name\":\"Rahul Bose\",\"yoe\":0,\"skills\":[\"LLM applications\",\"prompt engineering\",\"RAG\",\"ag","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":8,"name":"drop_candidate","reason":"c131 has the same keyword-only profile as c060 (game developer, skills list, \"passionate about\" line) with no LLM build or evaluation work.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"dropped\":\"c131\",\"remainingWithEvidence\":0}","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":9,"name":"search_candidates","reason":"Both llm-apps leads were keyword-only and dropped; reformulating m1 toward shipped LLM features and chatbots to surface candidates describing real builds.","ok":true,"summary":"12 hits, top undefined","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":10,"name":"get_profile","reason":"c100 snippet cites grounded chat assistants and rubric-graded model outputs, a possible match for both m1 and m2; verifying against the full profile.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"id\":\"c100\",\"name\":\"Ravi Bose\",\"yoe\":4,\"skills\":[\"grounded chat assistants over internal docs\",\"gra","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":11,"name":"cite_evidence","reason":"c100 profile describes shipped grounded chat assistants and a graded held-out evaluation set, which supports m1, m2 and the RAG and agent nice-to-haves.","ok":true,"summary":"rejected: undefined","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":12,"name":"get_profile","reason":"Only one candidate (c100) is fully evidenced; c077 is a 5-year llm-apps profile with guardrails and tool-calling, so checking it for m1 and m2 evidence.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"id\":\"c077\",\"name\":\"Ivan Petrov\",\"yoe\":5,\"skills\":[\"guardrails and refusal handling\",\"LangGraph\",\"t","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":13,"name":"cite_evidence","reason":"c077 profile describes shipped grounded chat assistants and a graded held-out set gating releases, which supports m1 and m2.","ok":true,"summary":"rejected: undefined","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":14,"name":"get_profile","reason":"Two candidates are fully evidenced; c020 is a 6-year llm-apps profile from the reformulated search and may supply the third.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"id\":\"c020\",\"name\":\"Nora Sharma\",\"yoe\":6,\"skills\":[\"guardrails and refusal handling\",\"tool-calling ","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":15,"name":"cite_evidence","reason":"c020 profile describes a shipped grounded chat assistant with a graded held-out set gating releases, which supports m1 and m2.","ok":true,"summary":"rejected: undefined","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":16,"name":"search_candidates","reason":"Three of five targets are fully evidenced; the evaluation must-have is the scarcest, so I'm trying eval-pipeline wording to find two more candidates with release-gating LLM evals.","ok":true,"summary":"12 hits, top undefined","phase":"SOURCE"},{"n":17,"name":"create_assessment","reason":"Approved candidates have verified must-haves, so assessments target the nice-to-haves their profiles don't evidence: prompt engineering for all three, and RAG for c100 (its RAG citation was rejected).","ok":true,"summary":"{\"created\":3,\"assessments\":[{\"candidate_id\":\"c077\",\"assessment_id\":\"asm_w030g8_c077\",\"targets\":[\"pro","phase":"ASSESS"},{"n":18,"name":"send_assessment","reason":"Assessments were created for all three HR-approved candidates (c077, c020, c100), so they can now be sent.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"sent\":3,\"results\":[{\"candidate_id\":\"c077\",\"messageId\":\"msg_mvw0471r_c077\",\"channel\":\"outbox\",\"stat","phase":"ASSESS"},{"n":19,"name":"collect_responses","reason":"All three assessments were sent (queued), so the next step is collecting the simulated answers for c077, c020 and c100.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"simulated\":true,\"collected\":3,\"responses\":[{\"candidate_id\":\"c077\",\"answers\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"prev","phase":"ASSESS"},{"n":20,"name":"run_code","reason":"Responses are collected for all three candidates; their JavaScript solve() answers now need to run against the hidden tests.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"results\":[{\"candidate_id\":\"c077\",\"passed\":2,\"total\":3},{\"candidate_id\":\"c020\",\"passed\":2,\"total\":3","phase":"ASSESS"},{"n":21,"name":"grade_answer","reason":"Sandbox results are in (each 2/3); the rubric grade is needed to combine with the code scores and check for disagreement.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"graded\":3,\"results\":[{\"candidate_id\":\"c077\",\"total\":0.3,\"parts\":[{\"kind\":\"short\",\"score\":0.3,\"conf","phase":"ASSESS"},{"n":22,"name":"advance_phase","reason":"All three approved candidates are assessed, graded, and unflagged (c100 0.63, c077 0.30, c020 0.25), so the ASSESS exit condition holds.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"phase\":\"AUDIT\",\"exitCondition\":\"fairness_audit has run on the current live shortlist\"}","phase":"AUDIT"},{"n":23,"name":"fairness_audit","reason":"Audit phase requires a fairness check on the live three-candidate shortlist (c077, c020, c100) before finalizing.","ok":true,"summary":"rho 0.5, overlap 1","phase":"AUDIT"},{"n":24,"name":"save_memory","reason":"Audit passed (no moves of 3+ places, top-5 overlap 100%); recording role lessons for future sessions.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"saved\":true}","phase":"AUDIT"},{"n":25,"name":"advance_phase","reason":"The fairness audit has run on the live shortlist (rho 0.5, top-5 overlap 100%, no moves of 3+ places), and the memory note is saved, so the AUDIT exit condition holds.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"phase\":\"FINALIZE\",\"exitCondition\":\"finalize_shortlist succeeded\"}","phase":"FINALIZE"},{"n":26,"name":"finalize_shortlist","reason":"Three candidates are fully evidenced on both must-haves and passed the audit with no blind-ranking flag. Scores use the 0.6 semantic and 0.4 assessment weighting, so the final ranking is c100, c077, c020.","ok":true,"summary":"{\"done\":true,\"ranked\":[\"c100\",\"c077\",\"c020\"]}","phase":"DONE"}],"searches":[{"query":"built LLM-powered applications in production using OpenAI or open-source language models","mode":"semantic","view":"visible","k":12,"source":"pgvector","top":[{"id":"c060","score":0.699},{"id":"c147","score":0.698},{"id":"c131","score":0.688},{"id":"c028","score":0.68},{"id":"R_006800","score":0.674},{"id":"R_008315","score":0.673},{"id":"R_004942","score":0.671},{"id":"R_003410","score":0.671},{"id":"R_005662","score":0.671},{"id":"R_004583","score":0.671}]},{"query":"evaluation harness for LLM outputs with golden datasets, LLM-as-judge, and regression metrics","mode":"semantic","view":"visible","k":12,"source":"pgvector","top":[{"id":"c147","score":0.685},{"id":"c156","score":0.683},{"id":"R_006148","score":0.677},{"id":"c040","score":0.674},{"id":"R_006882","score":0.674},{"id":"c122","score":0.671},{"id":"R_008635","score":0.669},{"id":"c117","score":0.669},{"id":"c049","score":0.668},{"id":"R_008526","score":0.667}]},{"query":"shipped a customer-facing chatbot or copilot on top of a large language model API, owning prompts, tool calls and latency","mode":"semantic","view":"visible","k":12,"source":"pgvector","top":[{"id":"c077","score":0.677},{"id":"c100","score":0.659},{"id":"c020","score":0.652},{"id":"c141","score":0.648},{"id":"R_005818","score":0.645},{"id":"c025","score":0.643},{"id":"c124","score":0.641},{"id":"c034","score":0.639},{"id":"c066","score":0.638},{"id":"R_003873","score":0.634}]},{"query":"built offline eval pipeline for LLM features with held-out sets gating releases and regression tracking","mode":"semantic","view":"visible","k":12,"source":"pgvector","top":[{"id":"c147","score":0.731},{"id":"c132","score":0.693},{"id":"c160","score":0.693},{"id":"c064","score":0.691},{"id":"c163","score":0.683},{"id":"c043","score":0.672},{"id":"c178","score":0.667},{"id":"c081","score":0.663},{"id":"c011","score":0.661},{"id":"c138","score":0.657}]}],"candidates":{"c060":{"id":"c060","name":"Ravi Sharma","semantic":0.698598433042451,"seen":1,"coverage":{"m1":0.8408423882366644,"m2":0.650019056437124,"n1":0.8188723719119757,"n2":0.5864402093573472,"n3":0.6284009087233628},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[{"requirementId":"m1","quote":"Passionate about LLM applications, prompt engineering and RAG.","reason":"does not show real experience with \"LLM applications\" (claude, 0.85)"},{"requirementId":"m2","quote":"Keywords: LLM applications, prompt engineering, RAG, agents, evaluation.","reason":"does not show real experience with \"evaluation\" (claude, 0.85)"}],"flags":[],"dropped":"Keyword-only skill claims for both must-haves; profile shows game development, no LLM build or evaluation work."},"c147":{"id":"c147","name":"Ivan Desai","semantic":0.730515675981171,"seen":3,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c131":{"id":"c131","name":"Rahul Bose","semantic":0.687577020418239,"seen":1,"coverage":{"m1":0.8408423882366644,"m2":0.650019056437124,"n1":0.8188723719119757,"n2":0.5864402093573472,"n3":0.6284009087233628},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"dropped":"m1 and m2 supported only by keyword lists and a self-description; no concrete LLM application or evaluation work described."},"c028":{"id":"c028","name":"Leon Tanaka","semantic":0.679684841538949,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_006800":{"id":"R_006800","name":"Tomas Verma","semantic":0.674339019937053,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_008315":{"id":"R_008315","name":"Daniel Verma","semantic":0.673329397040246,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_004942":{"id":"R_004942","name":"Nikhil Verma","semantic":0.671487930023157,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_003410":{"id":"R_003410","name":"Dev Verma","semantic":0.671392957651292,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_005662":{"id":"R_005662","name":"Rohan Verma","semantic":0.671171505977566,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_004583":{"id":"R_004583","name":"Harsh Verma","semantic":0.671050671682392,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c141":{"id":"c141","name":"Maya Pillai","semantic":0.669483364635736,"seen":2,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c005":{"id":"c005","name":"Karthik Silva","semantic":0.668321212034297,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c156":{"id":"c156","name":"Ishita Nguyen","semantic":0.682602007233978,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_006148":{"id":"R_006148","name":"Yusuf Haddad","semantic":0.676762242510186,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c040":{"id":"c040","name":"Yuki Nguyen","semantic":0.674341125511459,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_006882":{"id":"R_006882","name":"Daniel Silva","semantic":0.674119548866996,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c122":{"id":"c122","name":"Yuki Haddad","semantic":0.670543988661289,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_008635":{"id":"R_008635","name":"Aisha Verma","semantic":0.669199805068942,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c117":{"id":"c117","name":"Varun Mukherjee","semantic":0.669056435497297,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c049":{"id":"c049","name":"Aditya Novak","semantic":0.667689680870243,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_008526":{"id":"R_008526","name":"Marcus Verma","semantic":0.6667556961404,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_005406":{"id":"R_005406","name":"Ravi Verma","semantic":0.665943344865354,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_008465":{"id":"R_008465","name":"Elena Haddad","semantic":0.665809928987955,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c077":{"id":"c077","name":"Ivan Petrov","semantic":0.676809089944599,"seen":1,"coverage":{"m1":0.5852638793318984,"m2":0.5680731270503583,"n1":0.6372660544259388,"n2":0.4771030536327021,"n3":0.5241399354942823},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":254,"end":414,"quote":"Shipped grounded chat assistants end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls behind human approval, and a graded held-out set that gates every release.","strength":"strong","basis":"implied","by":"claude","confidence":0.95},{"requirementId":"m2","start":13,"end":71,"quote":"Practitioner working on graded model outputs with rubrics.","strength":"partial","basis":"implied","by":"claude","confidence":0.85},{"requirementId":"n2","start":555,"end":650,"quote":"Built a grounded chat assistant over internal docs with citation spans checked against sources.","strength":"strong","basis":"implied","by":"claude","confidence":0.93},{"requirementId":"n3","start":415,"end":495,"quote":"Shipped tool-calling workflows with human approval before anything irreversible.","strength":"partial","basis":"implied","by":"claude","confidence":0.85}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_w030g8_c077","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"A prompt that works well on your test set starts failing after a model version upgrade. Describe how you would find out what changed and what you would do about it. Keep the answer to a few short paragraphs.","rubric":"A good answer separates the prompt from the model: it proposes re-running a fixed evaluation set against old and new versions, comparing outputs case by case, and checking for format or instruction-following regressions. It then suggests a concrete fix such as tightening instructions, adding examples, pinning the model version, or adding output validation, and mentions keeping prompts under version control.","targets":["prompt engineering"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"You are asked to build a prompt that extracts invoice fields (vendor, date, total, currency) from messy text, and the output is consumed by a billing system that rejects malformed JSON. Walk through how you would design the prompt and how you would make sure it behaves reliably in production.","rubric":"A strong response specifies an explicit output schema with field types and a rule for missing values, uses few-shot examples that include edge cases such as multiple totals or unusual date formats, and asks for JSON only. It also describes validating output against the schema, retrying or flagging failures, and measuring field-level accuracy on a labelled sample before release.","targets":["prompt engineering"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string and returns a string. The input is a prompt template containing placeholders written as {{name}}, followed by a line containing the separator '---', followed by a JSON object mapping names to values. Replace every placeholder with the matching value. If a placeholder has no matching value, leave it unchanged. Return the filled template as a string. Example: input 'Hello {{name}}, you owe {{amount}}.\\n---\\n{\"name\":\"Ana\",\"amount\":\"5\"}' returns 'Hello Ana, you owe 5.'","rubric":"A correct solution splits on the first '---' line, parses the JSON, and uses a global regex replace on /\\{\\{(\\w+)\\}\\}/g with a callback that returns the value when the key exists and the original match otherwise. Watch for keys that are not own properties of the object, and for placeholders repeated in the template.","targets":["prompt engineering"],"tests":[{"input":"Hello {{name}}, you owe {{amount}}.\n---\n{\"name\":\"Ana\",\"amount\":\"5\"}","expected":"Hello Ana, you owe 5."},{"input":"{{a}} and {{a}} and {{b}}\n---\n{\"a\":\"x\"}","expected":"x and x and {{b}}"},{"input":"Total: {{total}}\n---\n{\"total\":\"0\",\"toString\":\"bad\"}","expected":"Total: 0"}]}],"emailSubject":"Next step for the LLM Application Engineer role","emailBody":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the LLM Application Engineer (mid) role. We would like to learn more about your prompt engineering work before the next stage.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small coding task in JavaScript. The coding task has a function called solve(input) and includes three test cases.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes in total. Work on it in one sitting if you can.\n\nReply to this email when you have finished, or if you have any questions about the tasks.\n\nThe hiring team","unverified":["prompt engineering"]},"sent":{"channel":"outbox","at":1793426975919},"responses":[{"kind":"short","answer":"I'd check what changed in the model and in our prompt. Usually I'd rerun the held-out set and look at which cases fail. I'd also compare outputs from the old and new versions and try to find a pattern. Then I'd tweak the prompt or adjust the eval to get it working again, and make sure nothing else broke."},{"kind":"scenario","answer":"I'd start by writing a prompt that asks the model to return the vendor, date, total, and currency as JSON. I'd include a few examples and tell it to return only JSON. For reliability, I'd run it on a bunch of sample invoices and fix any problems I see. Before release I'd check that the output parses and matches what billing expects, and I'd add some retries if it fails. Then I'd monitor it in production and handle errors when they come up."},{"kind":"code","answer":"function solve(input) {\n  const parts = input.split('---');\n  const template = parts[0];\n  const data = JSON.parse(parts[1]);\n  let out = template;\n  for (const k in data) {\n    out = out.replace('{{' + k + '}}', data[k]);\n  }\n  return out;\n}"}],"_tests":{"passed":2,"total":3,"results":[{"input":"Hello {{name}}, you owe {{amount}}.\n---\n{\"name\":\"Ana\",\"amount\":\"5\"}","expected":"Hello Ana, you owe 5.","got":"Hello Ana, you owe 5.\n","pass":true},{"input":"{{a}} and {{a}} and {{b}}\n---\n{\"a\":\"x\"}","expected":"x and x and {{b}}","got":"x and {{a}} and {{b}}\n","pass":false},{"input":"Total: {{total}}\n---\n{\"total\":\"0\",\"toString\":\"bad\"}","expected":"Total: 0","got":"Total: 0\n","pass":true}]},"grade":{"total":0.3,"parts":[{"kind":"short","score":0.3,"confidence":0.8,"note":"Covers re-running the eval and comparing old and new outputs, but never mentions format or instruction-following checks, pinning, output validation, or version control, and suggests adjusting the eval, which is a weak remedy."},{"kind":"scenario","score":0.25,"confidence":0.8,"note":"Vague on the schema: no field types, no missing-value rule, and no edge-case examples such as multiple totals or unusual dates. It mentions parse checks and retries but gives no field-level accuracy measurement on a labelled sample."},{"kind":"code","score":0.35,"confidence":0.85,"note":"Splits on any '---' rather than the separator line, and uses a string replace that only substitutes the first occurrence, so repeated placeholders stay unfilled. This matches the 2/3 hidden-test result; the for...in loop also lacks an own-property check and the regex approach.","testsPassed":2,"testsTotal":3}],"regraded":false},"final":0.5261},"c100":{"id":"c100","name":"Ravi Bose","semantic":0.659115234560777,"seen":1,"coverage":{"m1":0.5852638793318984,"m2":0.6112032142396415,"n1":0.6739711815989836,"n2":0.4993606844198943,"n3":0.5241399354942823},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":445,"end":605,"quote":"Shipped grounded chat assistants end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls behind human approval, and a graded held-out set that gates every release.","strength":"strong","basis":"implied","by":"claude","confidence":0.95},{"requirementId":"m2","start":263,"end":363,"quote":"Projects: Graded model outputs with rubrics and a held-out set, and tracked regressions per release.","strength":"strong","basis":"implied","by":"claude","confidence":0.9},{"requirementId":"n3","start":364,"end":444,"quote":"Shipped tool-calling workflows with human approval before anything irreversible.","strength":"partial","basis":"implied","by":"claude","confidence":0.85}],"failedClaims":[{"requirementId":"n2","quote":"Shipped grounded chat assistants end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls behind human approval, and a graded held-out set that gates every release.","reason":"does not show real experience with \"RAG\" (local, 0.80)"}],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_w030g8_c100","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"A retrieval-augmented chatbot answers questions about internal policy documents. Users report that answers are sometimes confidently wrong even though the correct text exists in the index. Name two likely causes in the retrieval or chunking pipeline and one concrete change you would make to address each.","rubric":"A good answer names specific failure modes such as chunks split mid-sentence or losing their heading context, embedding mismatch between query and document phrasing, or too few retrieved chunks reaching the prompt. Each cause is paired with a concrete fix, such as overlapping or section-aware chunking, hybrid keyword and vector search, reranking, or raising top-k with a relevance threshold. Two or three sentences is enough.","targets":["RAG"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"You are asked to build a prompt that extracts structured invoice fields (vendor, date, total, currency) from messy OCR text. The model sometimes returns prose around the JSON, and sometimes invents a total when none is present. Describe how you would change the prompt and the surrounding code to fix both problems, and how you would check that the fix worked.","rubric":"A strong answer specifies a strict output contract, such as a JSON schema with an explicit instruction to return null for missing fields, plus few-shot examples that include a missing-field case. It adds code-side validation and a retry or reject path for malformed output. It describes a small labelled test set with fields checked individually to measure the change. Three to five sentences is appropriate.","targets":["prompt engineering"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string and returns a string. The input is a template containing placeholders written as {{name}}, followed by a line containing 'VARS:' and a JSON object of values, separated by a newline. Replace each placeholder with the matching value from the JSON object. If a placeholder has no matching key, leave it unchanged. Return the filled template with no other changes.\n\nExample input:\nHello {{name}}, your code is {{code}}.\nVARS:{\"name\":\"Ana\",\"code\":\"X9\"}\n\nExample output:\nHello Ana, your code is X9.","rubric":"A correct answer splits the input on the first newline, parses the JSON after 'VARS:', and uses a regex replace with a callback that returns the value when the key exists and the original match otherwise. It should convert non-string values with String() and avoid repeated naive string replacement that could re-substitute inside inserted values. Under 15 minutes for an experienced engineer.","targets":["prompt engineering"],"tests":[{"input":"Hello {{name}}, your code is {{code}}.\nVARS:{\"name\":\"Ana\",\"code\":\"X9\"}","expected":"Hello Ana, your code is X9."},{"input":"Missing {{a}} and {{b}}.\nVARS:{\"b\":\"yes\"}","expected":"Missing {{a}} and yes."},{"input":"Count {{n}} and {{n}}.\nVARS:{\"n\":3}","expected":"Count 3 and 3."}]}],"emailSubject":"Assessment for the LLM Application Engineer role","emailBody":"Hi,\n\nThank you for applying for the LLM Application Engineer (mid) role. We would like you to complete a short assessment before we go further. It has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript function.\n\nThe short answer and scenario are about retrieval and prompt design. The coding task is a single function named solve. Please answer in plain text or code in your own editor, and include the function as written.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes in total. You do not need to be perfect. We would rather see how you reason through each problem.\n\nPlease reply to this email with your answers by the date we agreed.\n\nThe hiring team","unverified":["prompt engineering","RAG"]},"sent":{"channel":"outbox","at":1793426975919},"responses":[{"kind":"short","answer":"Two likely causes are chunking that splits a policy clause across chunks, so neither chunk alone holds the answer, and weak retrieval ranking, where the embedding model favors semantically similar but wrong sections over the exact clause. For chunking, I'd switch to overlapping chunks of about 400 tokens with a 15% overlap, and keep section headers attached to each chunk. For ranking, I'd add a hybrid BM25 plus embedding search and a cross-encoder reranker on the top 20 results."},{"kind":"scenario","answer":"I'd tighten the prompt by putting the schema in a system message and adding an explicit instruction to return only a JSON object with no surrounding text. I'd also include one few-shot example with a missing total, where the output has total set to null. In the code, I'd call the model with JSON mode if the provider supports it, strip any leading or trailing text by extracting the first balanced brace block, and validate with a schema library. If total is null in the output, I'd keep it null rather than retry. To check the fix, I'd build a labeled set of about 150 invoices, including some with no total and some with noisy OCR, and track parse success rate and hallucinated-total rate per release. I'd gate releases on those numbers. I'd also spot-check a random sample by hand, since the labels can be off."},{"kind":"code","answer":"function solve(input) {\n  const idx = input.indexOf('\\nVARS:');\n  const template = input.slice(0, idx);\n  const vars = JSON.parse(input.slice(idx + 6));\n  let out = template;\n  for (const key of Object.keys(vars)) {\n    out = out.replace('{{' + key + '}}', String(vars[key]));\n  }\n  return out;\n}"}],"_tests":{"passed":2,"total":3,"results":[{"input":"Hello {{name}}, your code is {{code}}.\nVARS:{\"name\":\"Ana\",\"code\":\"X9\"}","expected":"Hello Ana, your code is X9.","got":"Hello Ana, your code is X9.","pass":true},{"input":"Missing {{a}} and {{b}}.\nVARS:{\"b\":\"yes\"}","expected":"Missing {{a}} and yes.","got":"Missing {{a}} and yes.","pass":true},{"input":"Count {{n}} and {{n}}.\nVARS:{\"n\":3}","expected":"Count 3 and 3.","got":"Count 3 and {{n}}.","pass":false}]},"grade":{"total":0.633,"parts":[{"kind":"short","score":0.9,"confidence":0.85,"note":"Names two specific causes (clause split across chunks, embedding ranking favoring similar but wrong sections) with concrete fixes (overlapping chunks with headers, hybrid search plus reranker), matching the rubric closely."},{"kind":"scenario","score":0.7,"confidence":0.75,"note":"Covers a strict schema, null-for-missing instruction, a missing-field few-shot example, code validation, and a labelled test set, but has no explicit retry or reject path for malformed output and no per-field metrics, and runs longer than the suggested length."},{"kind":"code","score":0.3,"confidence":0.85,"note":"Hidden tests passed only 2 of 3. The code uses string replace with a single pattern, so it substitutes only the first occurrence of each repeated placeholder, and it sequentially replaces keys, so inserted values can be re-substituted. It does not use a regex callback as the rubric expects.","testsPassed":2,"testsTotal":3}],"regraded":false},"final":0.6487},"c020":{"id":"c020","name":"Nora Sharma","semantic":0.652418294693224,"seen":1,"coverage":{"m1":0.5486938413450807,"m2":0.5484804021710208,"n1":0.6372660544259388,"n2":0.48856349085923073,"n3":0.5241399354942823},"evidence":[{"requirementId":"m1","start":351,"end":511,"quote":"Shipped grounded chat assistants end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls behind human approval, and a graded held-out set that gates every release.","strength":"strong","basis":"implied","by":"claude","confidence":0.95},{"requirementId":"m2","start":512,"end":602,"quote":"Graded model outputs with rubrics and a held-out set, and tracked regressions per release.","strength":"strong","basis":"implied","by":"claude","confidence":0.93},{"requirementId":"n2","start":255,"end":350,"quote":"Built a grounded chat assistant over internal docs with citation spans checked against sources.","strength":"strong","basis":"implied","by":"claude","confidence":0.93},{"requirementId":"n3","start":603,"end":683,"quote":"Shipped tool-calling workflows with human approval before anything irreversible.","strength":"partial","basis":"implied","by":"claude","confidence":0.85}],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[],"assessment":{"id":"asm_w030g8_c020","questions":[{"kind":"short","prompt":"A prompt that works well on your test set starts failing after a model version upgrade. Describe how you would find out what changed and what you would do about it. Keep the answer to a few short paragraphs.","rubric":"A good answer separates the prompt from the model: it proposes re-running a fixed evaluation set against old and new versions, comparing outputs case by case, and checking for format or instruction-following regressions. It then suggests a concrete fix such as tightening instructions, adding examples, pinning the model version, or adding output validation, and mentions keeping prompts under version control.","targets":["prompt engineering"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"scenario","prompt":"You are asked to build a prompt that extracts invoice fields (vendor, date, total, currency) from messy text, and the output is consumed by a billing system that rejects malformed JSON. Walk through how you would design the prompt and how you would make sure it behaves reliably in production.","rubric":"A strong response specifies an explicit output schema with field types and a rule for missing values, uses few-shot examples that include edge cases such as multiple totals or unusual date formats, and asks for JSON only. It also describes validating output against the schema, retrying or flagging failures, and measuring field-level accuracy on a labelled sample before release.","targets":["prompt engineering"],"tests":[]},{"kind":"code","prompt":"Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string and returns a string. The input is a prompt template containing placeholders written as {{name}}, followed by a line containing the separator '---', followed by a JSON object mapping names to values. Replace every placeholder with the matching value. If a placeholder has no matching value, leave it unchanged. Return the filled template as a string. Example: input 'Hello {{name}}, you owe {{amount}}.\\n---\\n{\"name\":\"Ana\",\"amount\":\"5\"}' returns 'Hello Ana, you owe 5.'","rubric":"A correct solution splits on the first '---' line, parses the JSON, and uses a global regex replace on /\\{\\{(\\w+)\\}\\}/g with a callback that returns the value when the key exists and the original match otherwise. Watch for keys that are not own properties of the object, and for placeholders repeated in the template.","targets":["prompt engineering"],"tests":[{"input":"Hello {{name}}, you owe {{amount}}.\n---\n{\"name\":\"Ana\",\"amount\":\"5\"}","expected":"Hello Ana, you owe 5."},{"input":"{{a}} and {{a}} and {{b}}\n---\n{\"a\":\"x\"}","expected":"x and x and {{b}}"},{"input":"Total: {{total}}\n---\n{\"total\":\"0\",\"toString\":\"bad\"}","expected":"Total: 0"}]}],"emailSubject":"Next step for the LLM Application Engineer role","emailBody":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the LLM Application Engineer (mid) role. We would like to learn more about your prompt engineering work before the next stage.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small coding task in JavaScript. The coding task has a function called solve(input) and includes three test cases.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes in total. Work on it in one sitting if you can.\n\nReply to this email when you have finished, or if you have any questions about the tasks.\n\nThe hiring team","unverified":["prompt engineering"]},"sent":{"channel":"outbox","at":1793426975919},"responses":[{"kind":"short","answer":"I'd check whether the prompt or the model changed, then look at a few failing examples. Usually the new version formats output a bit differently or follows instructions more loosely, so I'd tweak the prompt and rerun the test set. If it still fails, I'd see whether the examples need updating."},{"kind":"scenario","answer":"I'd write a prompt that asks the model to return only JSON with vendor, date, total, and currency keys, and give it a sample input and output. Then I'd parse the response and fix any formatting problems before sending it to billing. To make it reliable in production, I'd run it on some test invoices and check the results before shipping."},{"kind":"code","answer":"function solve(input) {\n  const parts = input.split('---');\n  const template = parts[0];\n  const vals = JSON.parse(parts[1]);\n  return template.replace(/{{(\\w+)}}/g, (m, k) => vals[k]);\n}"}],"_tests":{"passed":2,"total":3,"results":[{"input":"Hello {{name}}, you owe {{amount}}.\n---\n{\"name\":\"Ana\",\"amount\":\"5\"}","expected":"Hello Ana, you owe 5.","got":"Hello Ana, you owe 5.\n","pass":true},{"input":"{{a}} and {{a}} and {{b}}\n---\n{\"a\":\"x\"}","expected":"x and x and {{b}}","got":"x and x and undefined\n","pass":false},{"input":"Total: {{total}}\n---\n{\"total\":\"0\",\"toString\":\"bad\"}","expected":"Total: 0","got":"Total: 0\n","pass":true}]},"grade":{"total":0.25,"parts":[{"kind":"short","score":0.3,"confidence":0.8,"note":"Mentions rerunning the test set and tweaking the prompt, but never proposes a side-by-side old-vs-new comparison, format or instruction-following checks, pinning, output validation, or version control."},{"kind":"scenario","score":0.1,"confidence":0.85,"note":"No explicit schema with types, no missing-value rule, no edge-case few-shot examples, no retry or flagging for malformed JSON, and no field-level accuracy measurement before release."},{"kind":"code","score":0.35,"confidence":0.8,"note":"Hidden tests passed 2/3. Splitting on any '---' rather than a separate line is fragile, and missing keys return undefined, which inserts 'undefined' instead of leaving the placeholder. Inherited keys like 'constructor' are also not guarded with hasOwnProperty, so the callback is incorrect for unmatched placeholders.","testsPassed":2,"testsTotal":3}],"regraded":false},"final":0.4915},"R_005818":{"id":"R_005818","name":"Omar Iyer","semantic":0.645347358702408,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c025":{"id":"c025","name":"Pooja Pillai","semantic":0.647827793982652,"seen":2,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c124":{"id":"c124","name":"Kenji Petrov","semantic":0.641411969156758,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c034":{"id":"c034","name":"Divya Kulkarni","semantic":0.638550270035793,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c066":{"id":"c066","name":"Elena Bose","semantic":0.637680166873852,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_003873":{"id":"R_003873","name":"Kenji Pillai","semantic":0.634117485565414,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"R_005442":{"id":"R_005442","name":"Omar Nguyen","semantic":0.628775484184862,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c089":{"id":"c089","name":"Manish Ahmed","semantic":0.627825086946738,"seen":1,"coverage":{},"evidence":[],"failedClaims":[],"flags":[]},"c132":{"id":"c132","na