18},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","kind":"decision","node":"decide","payload":{"task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"no","confidence":0.72,"costUsd":0.00032,"input":{"question":"You have a Postgres table of about 50 million rows, each with a 768-dimension embedding stored using the pgvector extension. Approximate nearest-neighbor que…","rubric":"A strong answer names HNSW and IVFFlat as the main pgvector options and explains that HNSW usually gives better recall and query latency at the cost of slowe…","answer":"I'd probably go with HNSW since it's usually the fastest for queries. The build is slower and uses more memory, but it gives good recall. I'd start by tuning…"},"rationale":"The answer correctly identifies HNSW, its slower build and higher memory cost, and ef_search as a query-time knob. However, it never mentions IVFFlat or its build-time trade-offs (faster build, need for training data, list count). It also does not mention checking recall against exact search before tuning. Suggesting tuning m first is questionable because m is a build-time parameter that requires rebuilding the index. Overall it is partially correct but misses several expected qualities."},"seq":166,"ts":1793426017019},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","kind":"llm","node":"llm","payload":{"purpose":"grade","cached":false,"costUsd":0.0005,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ms":3954,"usage":{"input":1172,"output":762,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0}},"seq":167,"ts":1793426020973},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","kind":"llm","node":"llm","payload":{"purpose":"decide:short_answer_pass","cached":false,"costUsd":0.00018,"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","ms":1505,"usage":{"input":765,"output":213,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0}},"seq":168,"ts":1793426022478},{"runId":"run_mvvzhb93","kind":"note","node":"llm","payload":{"text":"Structured output for decision failed validation ([\n  {\n    \"code\": \"invalid_type\",\n    \"expected\": \"number\",\n    \"received\": \"string\",\n    \"path\": [\n      \"confidence\"\n ); retr