sk":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.7,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426102975,"input":{"sentence":"She would describe her strengths as: REST contract design, FastAPI services, PostgreSQL query tuning, pgvector and FAISS retrieval.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426106763,"input":{"text":"Aditya Novak. Builder focused on pgvector and faiss retrieval. Captains the men's football team at work.\nAditya studied at Greenhill Institute and has 6 years of experience. He would describe his strengths as: pgvector and FAISS retrieval, REST contract design, FastAPI services, dense passage ranking.\nProjects: Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints. Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set. Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms. Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426108675,"input":{"sentence":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426108686,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426108696,"input":{"sentence":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426108706,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Embeddings pipeline"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426110549,"input":{"text":"Elena Bose. Builder focused on sentence-encoder serving.\nElena studied at Carrow National Institute and has 8 years of experience. She would describe her strengths as: pgvector and FAISS retrieval, sentence-encoder serving, FastAPI services, dense passage ranking, REST contract design.\nProjects: Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set. Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms. Designed REST contracts for a dense passage ranking service used by three internal teams. Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426112864,"input":{"sentence":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.92,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426112875,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426112887,"input":{"sentence":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426112898,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Embeddings pipeline"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0.0000694,"counterfactualUsd":0.0000694,"ts":1793426113728,"input":{"sentence":"Designed REST contracts for a dense passage ranking service used by three internal teams.","requirement":"API design"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426115820,"input":{"text":"Hana Tanaka. Builder focused on dense passage ranking.\nHana studied at Greenhill Institute and has 6 years of experience. She would describe her strengths as: dense passage ranking, PostgreSQL query tuning, FastAPI services.\nProjects: Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms. Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches. Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set. Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426118243,"input":{"sentence":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426118255,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426118267,"input":{"sentence":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426118277,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Embeddings pipeline"}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0.0001457,"counterfactualUsd":0.0001457,"ts":1793426176550,"input":{"question":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","answer":"I would use a POST or GET search endpoint with a typed request body: `query` (text or vector reference), `limit` (default 20, hard cap 100), `cursor` (opaque string, not a raw offset), and `filters` as an object with an explicit allowlist of fields and operators. The response returns `items` (each with `id`, `score`, and a `metadata` object), plus `next_cursor` (null at the end) and `schema_version`. Cursors encode the last sort key plus a snapshot marker so paging stays stable under concurrent writes. Filters are validated against a versioned schema: unknown fields are rejected with a 400 that names the field, not silently ignored, and new filterable fields are added as optional so older clients keep working. Response fields only grow additively, so clients that ignore unknown keys keep working as the schema evolves."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.6,"costUsd":0.0002631,"counterfactualUsd":0.0002631,"ts":1793426180791,"input":{"question":"A Postgres table stores 50 million document chunks with a 768-dimension embedding column and a tenant_id column. Queries always filter by tenant_id and then need the 10 nearest neighbours by cosine distance. Describe the indexing approach you would use, the trade-offs you would weigh between index types, and one thing you would check in EXPLAIN ANALYZE to confirm the index is used.","rubric":"A strong answer proposes a pgvector HNSW or IVFFlat index on the embedding column, and considers tenant_id through a partial index, partitioning, or a composite approach rather than ignoring the filter. It names a real tradeoff such as build time and memory versus recall and query speed, and names a concrete EXPLAIN ANALYZE check such as confirming an index scan on the vector index with the expected row estimate.","answer":"I'd add an index on the embedding column so the nearest-neighbour search doesn't scan everything. For 50 million rows, I'd probably use pgvector with an HNSW index, since it gives good recall and latency. I'd also think about tenant_id, maybe with a partial index or a btree index on it. The trade-off is that HNSW uses more memory and takes longer to build than IVFFlat, but it's usually faster to query. To check that the index is used, I'd run EXPLAIN ANALYZE and look for an index scan in the plan."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.92,"costUsd":0.00014519999999999998,"counterfactualUsd":0.00014519999999999998,"ts":1793426186416,"input":{"question":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","answer":"I'd use a POST or GET search request with a query string, a `limit` (default 20, max 100) and an opaque `cursor` for pagination, plus a `filters` object whose keys are allow-listed and typed. The response would return `items` with `id`, `score`, and `metadata`, plus `next_cursor` and a `schema_version` field. Cursors would encode the last sort key rather than an offset, so inserts don't shift pages. For filter validation I'd validate against a versioned schema at the edge and reject unknown keys with a 400 that names the offending field, so new fields can be added without older clients silently changing results."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.92,"costUsd":0.0001715,"counterfactualUsd":0.0001715,"ts":1793426191550,"input":{"question":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","answer":"I would expose a GET /v1/search endpoint that takes a query string or vector reference, a filters object keyed by declared metadata fields, and a cursor plus limit for pagination. The response would return a results array of {id, score, metadata subset} plus a next_cursor that is opaque to clients, so I can change the underlying offset or keyset strategy later without breaking them. For pagination I would use keyset cursors over (score, id) rather than offsets, since offsets drift when the index is updated and get expensive at depth. For filters I would validate against a versioned schema at the gateway: unknown filter keys return a 400 with the list of supported fields, types and ranges are checked, and filters are normalized before they reach the index. To keep the API stable as the schema evolves, I would only add optional fields and filters, never change the meaning of existing ones, and document unknown-field handling as ignore-on-input and omit-on-output for clients that do not request them."}},{"runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.8,"costUsd":0.0001823,"counterfactualUsd":0.0001823,"ts":1793426196163,"input":{"question":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","answer":"I would take a page size and an opaque cursor as query params, and return a list of results with a next_cursor field. Filters would be passed as a small set of named metadata fields, validated with a Pydantic model so unknown fields get rejected. I would keep the response envelope simple so new fields can be added later without breaking clients."}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426234686,"input":{"line":"Senior Backend Engineer, Retrieval Platform"}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426234692,"input":{"line":"We are building a retrieval platform."}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426234703,"input":{"line":"You will own our vector search stack and the embeddings pipeline that feeds it, ship semantic search features, tune Postgres, write Python services, and lead API design for internal consumers."}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426234711,"input":{"line":"Must have: vector search, Python, Postgres."}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426234718,"input":{"line":"Nice to have: embeddings pipeline, API design."}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426234724,"input":{"line":"Seniority: senior."}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"yes","confidence":0.95,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426234724,"input":{"line":"Only female candidates under 28 from IIT or Northfield."}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"yes","confidence":0.95,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426234724,"input":{"line":"Must be unmarried."}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426238302,"input":{"text":"Ishita Nguyen. Builder focused on pgvector and faiss retrieval.\nIshita studied at Westbrook University and has 11 years of experience. She would describe her strengths as: REST contract design, dense passage ranking, sentence-encoder serving, FastAPI services, pgvector and FAISS retrieval.\nProjects: Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches. Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints. Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms. Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set."}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426240115,"input":{"sentence":"Built a FAISS index over 40