ewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426254920,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the Python ingestion and query services around the index, with asyncio workers and typed FastAPI endpoints.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0.0000872,"counterfactualUsd":0.0000872,"ts":1793426255989,"input":{"sentence":"PostgreSQL query tuning","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426256002,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Embeddings pipeline"}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426256011,"input":{"sentence":"Designed REST contracts for a dense passage ranking service used by three internal teams.","requirement":"API design"}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426302407,"input":{"question":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","answer":"I would use a POST or GET search endpoint with a typed request body: `query` (text or vector reference), `limit` (default 20, hard cap 100), `cursor` (opaque string, not a raw offset), and `filters` as an object with an explicit allowlist of fields and operators. The response returns `items` (each with `id`, `score`, and a `metadata` object), plus `next_cursor` (null at the end) and `schema_version`. Cursors encode the last sort key plus a snapshot marker so paging stays stable under concurrent writes. Filters are validated against a versioned schema: unknown fields are rejected with a 400 that names the field, not silently ignored, and new filterable fields are added as optional so older clients keep working. Response fields only grow additively, so clients that ignore unknown keys keep working as the schema evolves."}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.92,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426302408,"input":{"question":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","answer":"I'd use a POST or GET search request with a query string, a `limit` (default 20, max 100) and an opaque `cursor` for pagination, plus a `filters` object whose keys are allow-listed and typed. The response would return `items` with `id`, `score`, and `metadata`, plus `next_cursor` and a `schema_version` field. Cursors would encode the last sort key rather than an offset, so inserts don't shift pages. For filter validation I'd validate against a versioned schema at the edge and reject unknown keys with a 400 that names the offending field, so new fields can be added without older clients silently changing results."}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.92,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426302408,"input":{"question":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","answer":"I would expose a GET /v1/search endpoint that takes a query string or vector reference, a filters object keyed by declared metadata fields, and a cursor plus limit for pagination. The response would return a results array of {id, score, metadata subset} plus a next_cursor that is opaque to clients, so I can change the underlying offset or keyset strategy later without breaking them. For pagination I would use keyset cursors over (score, id) rather than offsets, since offsets drift when the index is updated and get expensive at depth. For filters I would validate against a versioned schema at the gateway: unknown filter keys return a 400 with the list of supported fields, types and ranges are checked, and filters are normalized before they reach the index. To keep the API stable as the schema evolves, I would only add optional fields and filters, never change the meaning of existing ones, and document unknown-field handling as ignore-on-input and omit-on-output for clients that do not request them."}},{"runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.8,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426302408,"input":{"question":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","answer":"I would take a page size and an opaque cursor as query params, and return a list of results with a next_cursor field. Filters would be passed as a small set of named metadata fields, validated with a Pydantic model so unknown fields get rejected. I would keep the response envelope simple so new fields can be added later without breaking clients."}},{"runId":"prewarm_1_mvvzprtg","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426303036,"input":{"line":"LLM Application Engineer"}},{"runId":"prewarm_1_mvvzprtg","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426303044,"input":{"line":"LLM application engineer: prompt engineering, RAG, agents, and evaluation."}},{"runId":"prewarm_1_mvvzprtg","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426303051,"input":{"line":"Must have: LLM applications, evaluation."}},{"runId":"prewarm_1_mvvzprtg","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426303064,"input":{"line":"Nice to have: prompt engineering, RAG, agents."}},{"runId":"prewarm_1_mvvzprtg","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426303071,"input":{"line":"Seniority: mid."}},{"runId":"prewarm_1_mvvzprtg","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426309850,"input":{"text":"Ravi Bose. Engineer who likes tool-calling workflows.\nRavi studied at Riverbend Polytechnic and has 4 years of experience. He would describe his strengths as: grounded chat assistants over internal docs, graded model outputs with rubrics, tool-calling workflows.\nProjects: Graded model outputs with rubrics and a held-out set, and tracked regressions per release. Shipped tool-calling workflows with human approval before anything irreversible. Shipped LLM applications end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls with approval, and an evaluation set that gates every release. Added guardrails and refusal handling for unsafe requests."}},{"runId":"prewarm_1_mvvzprtg","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0.0000895,"counterfactualUsd":0.0000895,"ts":1793426312899,"input":{"sentence":"Shipped LLM applications end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls with approval, and an evaluation set that gates every release.","requirement":"LLM applications"}},{"runId":"prewarm_1_mvvzprtg","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","labe