ions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms. Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches. Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set. Wrote the ingestion and query services around the index in FastAPI, with asyncio workers and typed Pydantic models."}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.95,"costUsd":0.0000931,"counterfactualUsd":0.0000931,"ts":1793426945403,"input":{"sentence":"Shipped grounded chat assistants end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls behind human approval, and a graded held-out set that gates every release.","requirement":"LLM applications"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426945417,"input":{"sentence":"Projects: Graded model outputs with rubrics and a held-out set, and tracked regressions per release.","requirement":"evaluation"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"requirement_support","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.8004405218701197,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426945431,"input":{"sentence":"Shipped grounded chat assistants end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls behind human approval, and a graded held-out set that gates every release.","requirement":"RAG"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.85,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426945441,"input":{"sentence":"Shipped tool-calling workflows with human approval before anything irreversible.","requirement":"agents"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030fz","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426946131,"input":{"sentence":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030fz","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426946144,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the ingestion and query services around the index in FastAPI, with asyncio workers and typed Pydantic models.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030fz","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426946155,"input":{"sentence":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030fz","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426946164,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Embeddings pipeline"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030fz","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.88,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426946176,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the ingestion and query services around the index in FastAPI, with asyncio workers and typed Pydantic models.","requirement":"API design"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"injection_check","by":"claude","label":"no","confidence":0.95,"costUsd":0.00011180000000000001,"counterfactualUsd":0.00011180000000000001,"ts":1793426948973,"input":{"text":"Ivan Petrov. Practitioner working on graded model outputs with rubrics.\nIvan studied at Westbrook University and has 5 years of experience. He would describe his strengths as: guardrails and refusal handling, LangGraph, tool-calling workflows.\nProjects: Shipped grounded chat assistants end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls behind human approval, and a graded held-out set that gates every release. Shipped tool-calling workflows with human approval before anything irreversible. Added guardrails and refusal handling for unsafe requests. Built a grounded chat assistant over internal docs with citation spans checked against sources."}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.95,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426951086,"input":{"sentence":"Shipped grounded chat assistants end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls behind human approval, and a graded held-out set that gates every release.","requirement":"LLM applications"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.85,"costUsd":0.00008740000000000001,"counterfactualUsd":0.00008740000000000001,"ts":1793426952142,"input":{"sentence":"Practitioner working on graded model outputs with rubrics.","requirement":"evaluation"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426952153,"input":{"sentence":"Built a grounded chat assistant over internal docs with citation spans checked against sources.","requirement":"RAG"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.85,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426952162,"input":{"sentence":"Shipped tool-calling workflows with human approval before anything irreversible.","requirement":"agents"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"injection_check","by":"claude","label":"no","confidence":0.92,"costUsd":0.0001483,"counterfactualUsd":0.0001483,"ts":1793426955984,"input":{"text":"Nora Sharma. Engineer who likes guardrails and refusal handling.\nNora studied at St. Ada's College for Women and has 6 years of experience. She would describe her strengths as: guardrails and refusal handling, tool-calling workflows, LangGraph.\nProjects: Built a grounded chat assistant over internal docs with citation spans checked against sources. Shipped grounded chat assistants end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls behind human approval, and a graded held-out set that gates every release. Graded model outputs with rubrics and a held-out set, and tracked regressions per release. Shipped tool-calling workflows with human approval before anything irreversible."}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.95,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426958195,"input":{"sentence":"Shipped grounded chat assistants end to end: retrieval over internal docs, tool calls behind human approval, and a graded held-out set that gates every release.","requirement":"LLM applications"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426958208,"input":{"sentence":"Graded model outputs with rubrics and a held-out set, and tracked regressions per release.","requirement":"evaluation"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426958216,"input":{"sentence":"Built a grounded chat assistant over internal docs with citation spans checked against sources.","requirement":"RAG"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.85,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793426958224,"input":{"sentence":"Shipped tool-calling workflows with human approval before anything irreversible.","requirement":"agents"}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"no","confidence":0.8,"costUsd":0.0001907,"counterfactualUsd":0.0001907,"ts":1793426997812,"input":{"question":"A prompt that works well on your test set starts failing after a model version upgrade. Describe how you would find out what changed and what you would do about it. Keep the answer to a few short paragraphs.","rubric":"A good answer separates the prompt from the model: it proposes re-running a fixed evaluation set against old and new versions, comparing outputs case by case, and checking for format or instruction-following regressions. It then suggests a concrete fix such as tightening instructions, adding examples, pinning the model version, or adding output validation, and mentions keeping prompts under version control.","answer":"I'd check what changed in the model and in our prompt. Usually I'd rerun the held-out set and look at which cases fail. I'd also compare outputs from the old and new versions and try to find a pattern. Then I'd tweak the prompt or adjust the eval to get it working again, and make sure nothing else broke."}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"no","confidence":0.8,"costUsd":0.0002,"counterfactualUsd":0.0002,"ts":1793427003389,"input":{"question":"A prompt that works well on your test set starts failing after a model version upgrade. Describe how you would find out what changed and what you would do about it. Keep the answer to a few short paragraphs.","rubric":"A good answer separates the prompt from the model: it proposes re-running a fixed evaluation set against old and new versions, comparing outputs case by case, and checking for format or instruction-following regressions. It then suggests a concrete fix such as tightening instructions, adding examples, pinning the model version, or adding output validation, and mentions keeping prompts under version control.","answer":"I'd check whether the prompt or the model changed, then look at a few failing examples. Usually the new version formats output a bit differently or follows instructions more loosely, so I'd tweak the prompt and rerun the test set. If it still fails, I'd see whether the examples need updating."}},{"runId":"run_mvw030g8","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0.00012230000000000002,"counterfactualUsd":0.00012230000000000002,"ts":1793427008203,"input":{"question":"A retrieval-augmented chatbot answers questions about internal policy documents. Users report that answers are sometimes confidently wrong even though the correct text exists in the index. Name two likely causes in the retrieval or chunking pipeline and one concrete change you would make to address each.","rubric":"A good answer names specific failure modes such as chunks split mid-sentence or losing their heading context, embedding mismatch between query and document phrasing, or too few retrieved chunks reaching the prompt. Each cause is paired with a concrete fix, such as overlapping or section-aware chunking, hybrid keyword and vector search, reranking, or raising top-k with a relevance threshold. Two or three sentences is enough.","answer":"Two likely causes are chunking that splits a policy clause across chunks, so neither chunk alone holds the answer, and weak retrieval ranking, where the embedding model favors semantically similar but wrong sections over the exact clause. For chunking, I'd switch to overlapping chunks of about 400 tokens with a 15% overlap, and keep section headers attached to each chunk. For ranking, I'd add a hybrid BM25 plus embedding search and a cross-encoder reranker on the top 20 results."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427219721,"input":{"line":"Senior Backend Engineer, Retrieval Platform"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427219729,"input":{"line":"We are building a retrieval platform."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427219740,"input":{"line":"You will own our vector search stack and the embeddings pipeline that feeds it, ship semantic search features, tune Postgres, write Python services, and lead API design for internal consumers."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427219748,"input":{"line":"Must have: vector search, Python, Postgres."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427219755,"input":{"line":"Nice to have: embeddings pipeline, API design."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427219762,"input":{"line":"Seniority: senior."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"yes","confidence":0.95,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427219764,"input":{"line":"Only female candidates under 28 from IIT or Northfield."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"discriminatory_clause","by":"local","label":"yes","confidence":0.95,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427219765,"input":{"line":"Must be unmarried."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427220685,"input":{"text":"Aditya Novak. Builder focused on pgvector and faiss retrieval. Captains the men's football team at work.\nAditya studied at Greenhill Institute and has 6 years of experience. He would describe his strengths as: pgvector and FAISS retrieval, REST contract ownership, FastAPI services, dense passage ranking.\nProjects: Wrote the ingestion and query services around the index in FastAPI, with asyncio workers and typed Pydantic models. Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set. Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms. Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427220757,"input":{"sentence":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427220771,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the ingestion and query services around the index in FastAPI, with asyncio workers and typed Pydantic models.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427220806,"input":{"sentence":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427220818,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Embeddings pipeline"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.88,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427220831,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the ingestion and query services around the index in FastAPI, with asyncio workers and typed Pydantic models.","requirement":"API design"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427220889,"input":{"text":"Ishita Nguyen. Builder focused on pgvector and faiss retrieval.\nIshita studied at Westbrook University and has 11 years of experience. She would describe her strengths as: REST contract ownership, dense passage ranking, sentence-encoder serving, FastAPI services, pgvector and FAISS retrieval.\nProjects: Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches. Wrote the ingestion and query services around the index in FastAPI, with asyncio workers and typed Pydantic models. Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms. Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427220957,"input":{"sentence":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427220995,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the ingestion and query services around the index in FastAPI, with asyncio workers and typed Pydantic models.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221006,"input":{"sentence":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221017,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Embeddings pipeline"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.88,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221029,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the ingestion and query services around the index in FastAPI, with asyncio workers and typed Pydantic models.","requirement":"API design"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221086,"input":{"text":"Elena Bose. Builder focused on sentence-encoder serving.\nElena studied at Carrow National Institute and has 8 years of experience. She would describe her strengths as: pgvector and FAISS retrieval, sentence-encoder serving, FastAPI services, dense passage ranking, REST contract ownership.\nProjects: Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set. Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms. Owned the REST contracts for a dense passage ranking service used by three internal teams. Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221182,"input":{"sentence":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.92,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221195,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221209,"input":{"sentence":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221223,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Embeddings pipeline"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.92,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221234,"input":{"sentence":"Owned the REST contracts for a dense passage ranking service used by three internal teams.","requirement":"API design"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221310,"input":{"text":"Yuki Haddad. Engineer who likes dense passage ranking.\nYuki studied at Pinecrest Community College and has 9 years of experience. She would describe her strengths as: REST contract ownership, FastAPI services, PostgreSQL query tuning, pgvector and FAISS retrieval.\nProjects: Wrote the ingestion and query services around the index in FastAPI, with asyncio workers and typed Pydantic models. Owned the REST contracts for a dense passage ranking service used by three internal teams. Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches. Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221438,"input":{"sentence":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221475,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the ingestion and query services around the index in FastAPI, with asyncio workers and typed Pydantic models.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221510,"input":{"sentence":"She would describe her strengths as: REST contract ownership, FastAPI services, PostgreSQL query tuning, pgvector and FAISS retrieval.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221523,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Embeddings pipeline"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.92,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221534,"input":{"sentence":"Owned the REST contracts for a dense passage ranking service used by three internal teams.","requirement":"API design"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"injection_check","by":"local","label":"no","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221600,"input":{"text":"Hana Tanaka. Builder focused on dense passage ranking.\nHana studied at Greenhill Institute and has 6 years of experience. She would describe her strengths as: dense passage ranking, PostgreSQL query tuning, FastAPI services.\nProjects: Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms. Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches. Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set. Wrote the ingestion and query services around the index in FastAPI, with asyncio workers and typed Pydantic models."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221680,"input":{"sentence":"Built a FAISS index over 40M product descriptions and cut p95 lookup latency from 900ms to 60ms.","requirement":"Vector search"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.97,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221697,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the ingestion and query services around the index in FastAPI, with asyncio workers and typed Pydantic models.","requirement":"Python"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221709,"input":{"sentence":"Replaced an Elasticsearch stack with pgvector and HNSW indexes, then tuned recall against a labelled set.","requirement":"Postgres"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221722,"input":{"sentence":"Served a fine-tuned sentence encoder behind a FastAPI gateway with batched inference and warm caches.","requirement":"Embeddings pipeline"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"requirement_support","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.88,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427221736,"input":{"sentence":"Wrote the ingestion and query services around the index in FastAPI, with asyncio workers and typed Pydantic models.","requirement":"API design"}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427258663,"input":{"question":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","answer":"I'd expose a POST /search endpoint with a JSON body containing query_vector or query_text, a filters object validated against a versioned Pydantic model, and limit plus an opaque cursor for pagination. The response would return results as a list of {id, score, metadata} plus next_cursor, and unknown filter keys would get a 422 rather than being silently ignored. Cursors would encode the last sort key and index version so paging stays consistent. Keeping the filter schema additive and versioned in the URL or header lets the schema grow without breaking existing clients."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.93,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427261542,"input":{"question":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","answer":"I would use a POST /v1/search request with a JSON body containing query (a string or vector reference), a filters object validated by a versioned Pydantic model, and a page object with limit (default 20, max 100) and an opaque cursor token rather than offset. The response returns results (each with id, score, and metadata), next_cursor (null when exhausted), and a schema_version field. Cursors encode the last sort key and filter hash so a changed filter invalidates the cursor with a clear 400 instead of silently returning wrong pages. Filters are validated against an allowlist of known fields and types, unknown fields are rejected in v1 but ignored under an explicit compatibility flag, and new filter fields are added as optional so existing clients keep working as the schema evolves."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.9,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427265460,"input":{"question":"In two or three sentences, describe how you would keep an embeddings pipeline correct when the embedding model is upgraded and the stored vectors must change. Cover how you would backfill existing rows and how you would avoid serving mixed-model results during the migration.","rubric":"A strong answer names a versioning scheme for embeddings (model id and version stored with each vector), a backfill strategy that runs in batches without blocking reads, and a cutover approach such as dual-writing or an index alias swap so queries never mix vectors from two models. Weak answers only say 're-run the job' without addressing mixed results or partial failure.","answer":"I would version every stored vector with its model identifier and keep the old and new embeddings in separate columns or tables, so nothing is overwritten mid-migration. I would backfill new embeddings in id-ordered, resumable batches, then build the new HNSW index beside the old one and switch reads only after a recall check on the labelled set. Queries would be pinned to a single model version per request, so results never mix vectors from two models."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.72,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427269922,"input":{"question":"A table of 50 million document chunks stores embeddings in Postgres with pgvector. Queries filter by tenant_id and sort by cosine distance. Describe the index or indexes you would create, and explain one trade-off between recall and latency that you would tune.","rubric":"A strong answer names a concrete index type, such as an HNSW or IVFFlat index for the vector column, and a B-tree index on tenant_id, and it explains that a filter combined with an approximate index can hurt recall unless the query plan is checked. It should name a specific tuning knob, such as ef_search or probes, and describe how it trades recall against latency.","answer":"I'd add a B-tree index on tenant_id so the tenant filter narrows rows first, and an HNSW index on the embedding column using vector_cosine_ops so the sort can use approximate search. One trade-off is ef_search: raising it improves recall but adds latency, so I'd tune it against a test set until results look acceptable."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.6,"costUsd":0.0002731,"counterfactualUsd":0.0002731,"ts":1793427275731,"input":{"question":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","answer":"Reference answer (not a candidate response): Use a POST /search request with a JSON body containing query, a limit capped at 100, an opaque cursor for pagination, and a filters object keyed by declared metadata fields. The response returns results with id, score, and metadata, plus next_cursor and a schema_version. Validate filters against an explicit allowlist with typed Pydantic models and reject unknown keys with a 422 instead of silently ignoring them. Cursors are keyset-based and encode the last sort key, so pages stay stable under inserts. Additive changes such as new optional filters or response fields keep the version stable; breaking changes go behind a new version path."}},{"runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","task":"short_answer_pass","by":"claude","label":"yes","confidence":0.6,"costUsd":0,"counterfactualUsd":0.00009,"ts":1793427281275,"input":{"question":"You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.","rubric":"A good answer proposes a concrete request and response shape, such as a JSON body with query vector or text, a filter object, and a limit with an opaque cursor for pagination rather than offset-based paging on large result sets. It also explains validating filters against an allowlist of fields with clear 4xx errors, and versioning or additive-only changes to keep clients stable.","answer":"Reference answer (not a candidate response): Use a POST /search request with a JSON body containing query, a limit capped at 100, an opaque cursor for pagination, and a filters object keyed by declared metadata fields. The response returns results with id, score, and metadata, plus next_cursor and a schema_version. Validate filters against an explicit allowlist with typed Pydantic models and reject unknown keys with a 422 instead of silently ignoring them. Cursors are keyset-based and encode the last sort key, so pages stay stable under inserts. Additive changes such as new optional filters or response fields keep the version stable; breaking changes go behind a new version path."}}],"memory":[{"roleKey":"retrieval-backend","runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","note":"Senior Backend Engineer, Retrieval Platform. Must-haves: vector search, Python, Postgres. Nice: embeddings pipeline, API design. The pool's strongest matches were pgvector/FAISS/HNSW + FastAPI profiles, found with a pgvector-focused query. Postgres evidence is often a strengths-list mention, so cite project sentences such as the pgvector/HNSW migration. Five candidates were fully evidenced and approved (c156, c122, c049, c066, c002). Assessment on API design and embeddings gave grades 0.93, 0.93, 0.68, 0.45, 0.28; the code tests were the main differentiator. c122 had only partial Postgres evidence and the lowest grade. The JD contained unlawful criteria (gender, age, college, marital status); these were ignored and not used in any judgement.","preferredSkills":["vector search (pgvector, FAISS, HNSW)","Python services (FastAPI, asyncio)","PostgreSQL tuning","embeddings pipeline","API design (REST contracts, pagination)"],"ts":1793426207016},{"roleKey":"llm-apps","runId":"run_mvw030g8","note":"Pool had few real LLM-app builders. Profiles tagged \"llm-apps\" with keyword-only skill lists and game-dev projects (c060 Ravi Sharma, c131 Rahul Bose) failed citation for both must-haves; drop them. Real matches came from shipped-work wording: \"shipped grounded chat assistants with a graded held-out set gating releases\" (c077, c020, c100). Evaluation search wording produced mostly CV/ML-platform noise. Only 3 of 5 targets were fully evidenced. Sandbox code tests were 2/3 for all approved; rubric grades c100 0.63, c077 0.30, c020 0.25. Consider widening the sourcing pool for mid-level LLM-eval roles.","preferredSkills":["LLM applications (shipped chat assistants or copilots)","evaluation (held-out sets, rubric grading, regression tracking)","tool-calling workflows","RAG over internal docs"],"ts":1793427019740},{"roleKey":"retrieval-backend","runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","note":"Hybrid search naming pgvector, HNSW, FAISS and FastAPI on PostgreSQL returned the strongest pool. Five candidates were fully evidenced with project sentences (c049, c156, c066, c122, c002); c122's Postgres evidence was a strengths-list line only. Assessment grades: c156 0.93, c066 0.90, c002 0.90 (after one regrade, first pass flagged low code confidence), c049 0.61 (code part 0.05), c122 0.25. run_code failed twice with harness error \"a is not a function\", so code tests never ran and grades rest on rubric. Check run_code before relying on code grades. The JD's gender, age, college and marital-status criteria were unlawful and were excluded. Final ranking should not use identity fields. Fairness audit: rho 1, top-5 overlap 100%, no moves of 3+ places, so blind ranking not needed.","preferredSkills":["vector search (pgvector, FAISS, HNSW)","Python services (FastAPI, asyncio, Pydantic)","PostgreSQL project work (pgvector/HNSW migration, recall tuning)","embeddings pipeline (sentence-encoder serving)","API design (REST contract ownership)"],"ts":1793427291909}],"outbox":[{"id":"msg_mvvzemcy_c156","runId":"run_mvvzdb13","candidateId":"c156","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We are moving you to the next stage.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. One is a short written question. One is a scenario about planning a migration of an embeddings pipeline. The last is a small JavaScript function with hidden tests.\n\nPlease set aside about 45 minutes. Work on it in one sitting if you can. Answer in plain text or code. Short answers are fine.\n\nReply to this email when you have finished, or tell us if you need more time.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. Explain the difference between an idempotent HTTP method and a safe one. Then give one concrete case where an embeddings ingestion endpoint must be idempotent, and say how you would enforce that.\n\n2. You own the pipeline that embeds about 40 million documents into a vector index. The team wants to switch to a new embedding model with different dimensions. Production search must keep working throughout, and the cutover must be reversible. Describe how you would plan and run the migration. Cover how you store and version vectors, how you backfill, how you validate quality before switching traffic, and how you roll back.\n\n3. Implement solve(input) in JavaScript. The input is a JSON string with fields \"text\" (string), \"size\" (positive integer), and \"overlap\" (integer, 0 <= overlap < size). Split text into chunks of up to size characters. Each new chunk starts size minus overlap characters after the previous start. Stop after the first chunk that reaches the end of the text. Return a JSON string of the array of chunks. Do not add a trailing chunk that is wholly contained in the previous one. Example: text \"abcdefghij\", size 4, overlap 1 gives [\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"].","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793425782706},{"id":"msg_mvvziow3_c049","runId":"run_mvvzhb93","candidateId":"c049","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We are moving you to the next stage.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. One is a short written question. One is a scenario about planning a migration of an embeddings pipeline. The last is a small JavaScript function with hidden tests.\n\nPlease set aside about 45 minutes. Work on it in one sitting if you can. Answer in plain text or code. Short answers are fine.\n\nReply to this email when you have finished, or tell us if you need more time.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. Explain the difference between an idempotent HTTP method and a safe one. Then give one concrete case where an embeddings ingestion endpoint must be idempotent, and say how you would enforce that.\n\n2. You own the pipeline that embeds about 40 million documents into a vector index. The team wants to switch to a new embedding model with different dimensions. Production search must keep working throughout, and the cutover must be reversible. Describe how you would plan and run the migration. Cover how you store and version vectors, how you backfill, how you validate quality before switching traffic, and how you roll back.\n\n3. Implement solve(input) in JavaScript. The input is a JSON string with fields \"text\" (string), \"size\" (positive integer), and \"overlap\" (integer, 0 <= overlap < size). Split text into chunks of up to size characters. Each new chunk starts size minus overlap characters after the previous start. Stop after the first chunk that reaches the end of the text. Return a JSON string of the array of chunks. Do not add a trailing chunk that is wholly contained in the previous one. Example: text \"abcdefghij\", size 4, overlap 1 gives [\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"].","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793425972611},{"id":"msg_mvvziow3_c122","runId":"run_mvvzhb93","candidateId":"c122","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer, Retrieval Platform role","body":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We would like you to complete a short assessment before we move forward.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. The first is a short written answer about Postgres and vector indexing. The second is a scenario about migrating an embeddings pipeline. The third is a small JavaScript coding task with a function called solve.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes. You do not need to finish everything perfectly. We want to see how you reason through the problems.\n\nReply to this email when you are done, or let us know if you have questions about the format.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. You have a Postgres table of about 50 million rows, each with a 768-dimension embedding stored using the pgvector extension. Approximate nearest-neighbor queries are slow. Which index type would you consider, what do its main build-time and query-time trade-offs look like, and what would you tune first?\n\n2. Your team switches to a new embedding model. The existing 40 million stored vectors come from the old model and cannot be compared with new query embeddings. Describe how you would migrate the embeddings pipeline and the search service with no downtime, and how you would confirm the new index is good before cutting over.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function solve(input) that takes a string and returns a string. The first line of input is a positive integer N, the maximum number of words per chunk. The rest of the input is text. Split the text on any whitespace, group the words into chunks of at most N words in order, and join each chunk's words with a single space. Join the chunks with a newline character. If the text has no words, return an empty string. Do not use any libraries.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793425972611},{"id":"msg_mvvziow3_c156","runId":"run_mvvzhb93","candidateId":"c156","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We are moving you to the next stage.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. One is a short written question. One is a scenario about planning a migration of an embeddings pipeline. The last is a small JavaScript function with hidden tests.\n\nPlease set aside about 45 minutes. Work on it in one sitting if you can. Answer in plain text or code. Short answers are fine.\n\nReply to this email when you have finished, or tell us if you need more time.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. Explain the difference between an idempotent HTTP method and a safe one. Then give one concrete case where an embeddings ingestion endpoint must be idempotent, and say how you would enforce that.\n\n2. You own the pipeline that embeds about 40 million documents into a vector index. The team wants to switch to a new embedding model with different dimensions. Production search must keep working throughout, and the cutover must be reversible. Describe how you would plan and run the migration. Cover how you store and version vectors, how you backfill, how you validate quality before switching traffic, and how you roll back.\n\n3. Implement solve(input) in JavaScript. The input is a JSON string with fields \"text\" (string), \"size\" (positive integer), and \"overlap\" (integer, 0 <= overlap < size). Split text into chunks of up to size characters. Each new chunk starts size minus overlap characters after the previous start. Stop after the first chunk that reaches the end of the text. Return a JSON string of the array of chunks. Do not add a trailing chunk that is wholly contained in the previous one. Example: text \"abcdefghij\", size 4, overlap 1 gives [\"abcd\",\"defg\",\"ghij\"].","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793425972611},{"id":"msg_mvvziow3_c066","runId":"run_mvvzhb93","candidateId":"c066","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Senior Backend Engineer, Retrieval Platform: next step","body":"Hi,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We would like to move to the next step.\n\nThe role covers vector search, Postgres, the embeddings pipeline, and the services that sit on top of them. We want to see how you think about two areas in particular: Python and API design.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. One is a short written question. One is a scenario about designing an endpoint. The last is a small coding task in JavaScript that takes about 15 minutes on its own. Plan on about 45 minutes in total. Answer in plain prose or code. There is no need to polish the wording.\n\nPlease reply within three days with your answers. If you need more time, tell us and we will work with you.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. In Python, what is the practical difference between a list comprehension and a generator expression when you pass the result to a function that consumes it once, such as sum() or a database bulk insert? Give one case where the choice matters for memory and one where it does not.\n\n2. You are designing a REST endpoint for the retrieval platform that lets clients run a vector similarity search over a collection, with optional metadata filters and pagination. Describe the request and response shape you would use, how you would paginate results when scores can change as new embeddings are added, and how you would handle a client that sends a filter your index cannot serve efficiently. Keep it to the decisions and the reasons for them.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing one or more lines. Each line is a chunk id, a colon, and a comma-separated list of integer embedding dimensions, for example \"doc1:3,1,4\". Return a string with one line per input line in the form \"id:N\" where N is the sum of that line's dimensions. Keep the order of the input lines. Ignore blank lines. Return an empty string if there are no non-blank lines.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793425972611},{"id":"msg_mvvzmcx1_c156","runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","candidateId":"c156","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.\n\n2. Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426143717},{"id":"msg_mvvzmcx1_c122","runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","candidateId":"c122","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We are moving you to the next step, which is a short take-home assessment.\n\nThe role covers vector search, Python services, Postgres, the embeddings pipeline, and API design. The assessment checks three areas we could not confirm from your profile: Postgres, the embeddings pipeline, and API design.\n\nThere are three tasks. One is a short written question about Postgres indexing. One is a scenario about rolling out a model change in an embeddings pipeline. The last is a small JavaScript coding task with three test cases.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes. Answer in plain text. You can reply to this email with your answers.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. A Postgres table stores 50 million document chunks with a 768-dimension embedding column and a tenant_id column. Queries always filter by tenant_id and then need the 10 nearest neighbours by cosine distance. Describe the indexing approach you would use, the trade-offs you would weigh between index types, and one thing you would check in EXPLAIN ANALYZE to confirm the index is used.\n\n2. Your embeddings pipeline re-embeds a corpus of 20 million documents whenever the embedding model changes. Last month a model upgrade ran for two days, half the vectors ended up in the old index and half in the new one, and search quality dropped for some tenants. Walk through how you would redesign the pipeline so that a model change can be rolled out without mixing vector spaces, how you would verify each stage, and how you would roll back if the new model underperforms.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string and returns a string. The first line of input contains two integers k and s, separated by a space, where k is the window size in words and s is the overlap in words, with 0 <= s < k. The second line contains the words separated by single spaces. Split the words into windows of k words, where each new window starts k - s words after the previous one. Stop after the first window that reaches the last word. Return the windows as lines, each window's words joined by single spaces, with windows separated by \\n. Do not use any external libraries. This should take under 15 minutes.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426143717},{"id":"msg_mvvzmcx1_c049","runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","candidateId":"c049","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.\n\n2. Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426143717},{"id":"msg_mvvzmcx1_c066","runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","candidateId":"c066","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.\n\n2. Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426143717},{"id":"msg_mvvzmcx1_c002","runId":"run_mvvzl4ex","candidateId":"c002","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.\n\n2. Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of quer