d solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426298386},{"id":"msg_mvvzpo9e_c066","runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","candidateId":"c066","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.\n\n2. Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426298386},{"id":"msg_mvvzpo9e_c002","runId":"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p","candidateId":"c002","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.\n\n2. Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426298386},{"id":"msg_mvvztex8_c030","runId":"prewarm_2_mvvzrdvj","candidateId":"c030","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Data Engineer (mid) assessment: three tasks, about 45 minutes","body":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Data Engineer (mid) role. We would like you to complete a short assessment before we talk further.\n\nIt has three tasks. The first is a short written question about Airflow. The second is a short scenario about rerunning a failed pipeline step. The third is a small JavaScript exercise that you write as a single function called solve.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes. You do not need to finish everything perfectly. We want to see how you reason.\n\nReply to this email with your answers when you are done. If anything in the instructions is unclear, ask us before you start.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. In Airflow, what is the difference between a DAG and a DAG run, and what is a task instance? Then explain why tasks should be idempotent when Airflow retries them.\n\n2. You own a daily Airflow DAG that loads yesterday's orders into a warehouse table. The load task fails halfway through, after writing about 60% of the rows. The on-call engineer asks you how to rerun it safely. Describe what you would check first, how you would rerun the DAG, and what you would change so the next failure does not cause the same problem.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function solve(input) that takes a string of dependency edges separated by spaces. Each edge looks like \"a>b\", meaning task a must run before task b. Return the task names in a valid run order as a comma-separated string with no spaces. When several tasks are ready at the same time, pick the alphabetically smallest first. If the edges contain a cycle, return \"cycle\". Only tasks that appear in an edge are included. An empty input returns an empty string.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426472908},{"id":"msg_mvvztex8_c051","runId":"prewarm_2_mvvzrdvj","candidateId":"c051","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Data Engineer (mid) take-home assessment","body":"Hi,\n\nThanks for applying for the Data Engineer (mid) role. We would like you to complete a short assessment so we can look more closely at your streaming work, which we could not confirm from your profile.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. The first is one short written answer about streaming concepts. The second is a scenario about designing a streaming job for late and duplicate events. The third is a small JavaScript function with three test cases.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes. Work on it alone and send back your answers and the code when you are done.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. In two or three sentences, explain the difference between event time and processing time in a streaming pipeline, and give one concrete case where using the wrong one would produce incorrect aggregates.\n\n2. You own a pipeline that reads clickstream events from a Kafka topic and writes hourly page-view counts per country to a warehouse table used by a daily dashboard. Some events arrive up to 30 minutes late, and occasionally the job restarts after a failure. Describe how you would design the streaming job so that the counts are correct and the restarts do not create duplicates. Name the specific mechanisms you would use and explain what you would check after a restart.\n\n3. Implement a function solve(input) that takes a string of newline-separated event records in the form 'timestamp_seconds,key,value' and returns a string. Records may arrive out of order. For each key, compute the sum of values inside tumbling 60-second windows, where a window starts at floor(timestamp/60)*60. Output one line per (window_start, key) pair, sorted by window_start ascending and then by key ascending, formatted as 'window_start,key,sum'. Ignore blank lines. Return the lines joined with newlines, with no trailing newline.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426472908},{"id":"msg_mvvztex8_c171","runId":"prewarm_2_mvvzrdvj","candidateId":"c171","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Data Engineer (mid) take-home assessment","body":"Hi,\n\nThanks for applying for the Data Engineer (mid) role. We would like you to complete a short assessment so we can look more closely at your streaming work, which we could not confirm from your profile.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. The first is one short written answer about streaming concepts. The second is a scenario about designing a streaming job for late and duplicate events. The third is a small JavaScript function with three test cases.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes. Work on it alone and send back your answers and the code when you are done.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. In two or three sentences, explain the difference between event time and processing time in a streaming pipeline, and give one concrete case where using the wrong one would produce incorrect aggregates.\n\n2. You own a pipeline that reads clickstream events from a Kafka topic and writes hourly page-view counts per country to a warehouse table used by a daily dashboard. Some events arrive up to 30 minutes late, and occasionally the job restarts after a failure. Describe how you would design the streaming job so that the counts are correct and the restarts do not create duplicates. Name the specific mechanisms you would use and explain what you would check after a restart.\n\n3. Implement a function solve(input) that takes a string of newline-separated event records in the form 'timestamp_seconds,key,value' and returns a string. Records may arrive out of order. For each key, compute the sum of values inside tumbling 60-second windows, where a window starts at floor(timestamp/60)*60. Output one line per (window_start, key) pair, sorted by window_start ascending and then by key ascending, formatted as 'window_start,key,sum'. Ignore blank lines. Return the lines joined with newlines, with no trailing newline.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426472908},{"id":"msg_mvvztex8_c118","runId":"prewarm_2_mvvzrdvj","candidateId":"c118","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Data Engineer (mid) assessment: three tasks, about 45 minutes","body":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Data Engineer (mid) role. We would like you to complete a short assessment before we talk further.\n\nIt has three tasks. The first is a short written question about Airflow. The second is a short scenario about rerunning a failed pipeline step. The third is a small JavaScript exercise that you write as a single function called solve.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes. You do not need to finish everything perfectly. We want to see how you reason.\n\nReply to this email with your answers when you are done. If anything in the instructions is unclear, ask us before you start.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. In Airflow, what is the difference between a DAG and a DAG run, and what is a task instance? Then explain why tasks should be idempotent when Airflow retries them.\n\n2. You own a daily Airflow DAG that loads yesterday's orders into a warehouse table. The load task fails halfway through, after writing about 60% of the rows. The on-call engineer asks you how to rerun it safely. Describe what you would check first, how you would rerun the DAG, and what you would change so the next failure does not cause the same problem.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function solve(input) that takes a string of dependency edges separated by spaces. Each edge looks like \"a>b\", meaning task a must run before task b. Return the task names in a valid run order as a comma-separated string with no spaces. When several tasks are ready at the same time, pick the alphabetically smallest first. If the edges contain a cycle, return \"cycle\". Only tasks that appear in an edge are included. An empty input returns an empty string.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426472908},{"id":"msg_mvw0471r_c077","runId":"run_mvw030g8","candidateId":"c077","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the LLM Application Engineer role","body":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the LLM Application Engineer (mid) role. We would like to learn more about your prompt engineering work before the next stage.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small coding task in JavaScript. The coding task has a function called solve(input) and includes three test cases.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes in total. Work on it in one sitting if you can.\n\nReply to this email when you have finished, or if you have any questions about the tasks.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. A prompt that works well on your test set starts failing after a model version upgrade. Describe how you would find out what changed and what you would do about it. Keep the answer to a few short paragraphs.\n\n2. You are asked to build a prompt that extracts invoice fields (vendor, date, total, currency) from messy text, and the output is consumed by a billing system that rejects malformed JSON. Walk through how you would design the prompt and how you would make sure it behaves reliably in production.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string and returns a string. The input is a prompt template containing placeholders written as {{name}}, followed by a line containing the separator '---', followed by a JSON object mapping names to values. Replace every placeholder with the matching value. If a placeholder has no matching value, leave it unchanged. Return the filled template as a string. Example: input 'Hello {{name}}, you owe {{amount}}.\\n---\\n{\"name\":\"Ana\",\"amount\":\"5\"}' returns 'Hello Ana, you owe 5.'","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426975919},{"id":"msg_mvw0471r_c020","runId":"run_mvw030g8","candidateId":"c020","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the LLM Application Engineer role","body":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the LLM Application Engineer (mid) role. We would like to learn more about your prompt engineering work before the next stage.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small coding task in JavaScript. The coding task has a function called solve(input) and includes three test cases.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes in total. Work on it in one sitting if you can.\n\nReply to this email when you have finished, or if you have any questions about the tasks.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. A prompt that works well on your test set starts failing after a model version upgrade. Describe how you would find out what changed and what you would do about it. Keep the answer to a few short paragraphs.\n\n2. You are asked to build a prompt that extracts invoice fields (vendor, date, total, currency) from messy text, and the output is consumed by a billing system that rejects malformed JSON. Walk through how you would design the prompt and how you would make sure it behaves reliably in production.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string and returns a string. The input is a prompt template containing placeholders written as {{name}}, followed by a line containing the separator '---', followed by a JSON object mapping names to values. Replace every placeholder with the matching value. If a placeholder has no matching value, leave it unchanged. Return the filled template as a string. Example: input 'Hello {{name}}, you owe {{amount}}.\\n---\\n{\"name\":\"Ana\",\"amount\":\"5\"}' returns 'Hello Ana, you owe 5.'","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426975919},{"id":"msg_mvw0471r_c100","runId":"run_mvw030g8","candidateId":"c100","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Assessment for the LLM Application Engineer role","body":"Hi,\n\nThank you for applying for the LLM Application Engineer (mid) role. We would like you to complete a short assessment before we go further. It has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript function.\n\nThe short answer and scenario are about retrieval and prompt design. The coding task is a single function named solve. Please answer in plain text or code in your own editor, and include the function as written.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes in total. You do not need to be perfect. We would rather see how you reason through each problem.\n\nPlease reply to this email with your answers by the date we agreed.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. A retrieval-augmented chatbot answers questions about internal policy documents. Users report that answers are sometimes confidently wrong even though the correct text exists in the index. Name two likely causes in the retrieval or chunking pipeline and one concrete change you would make to address each.\n\n2. You are asked to build a prompt that extracts structured invoice fields (vendor, date, total, currency) from messy OCR text. The model sometimes returns prose around the JSON, and sometimes invents a total when none is present. Describe how you would change the prompt and the surrounding code to fix both problems, and how you would check that the fix worked.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string and returns a string. The input is a template containing placeholders written as {{name}}, followed by a line containing 'VARS:' and a JSON object of values, separated by a newline. Replace each placeholder with the matching value from the JSON object. If a placeholder has no matching key, leave it unchanged. Return the filled template with no other changes.\n\nExample input:\nHello {{name}}, your code is {{code}}.\nVARS:{\"name\":\"Ana\",\"code\":\"X9\"}\n\nExample output:\nHello Ana, your code is X9.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793426975919},{"id":"msg_mvw09zzv_c049","runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","candidateId":"c049","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.\n\n2. Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793427246715},{"id":"msg_mvw09zzw_c156","runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","candidateId":"c156","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.\n\n2. Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793427246716},{"id":"msg_mvw09zzw_c066","runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","candidateId":"c066","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Senior Backend Engineer, Retrieval Platform: take-home assessment","body":"Hi,\n\nThank you for your time so far. We are moving you to the next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. The role covers vector search, Python services, Postgres, embeddings pipelines, and API design.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. One is a short written answer. One is a scenario about designing an embeddings pipeline. One is a small JavaScript function with three test cases. Please answer in plain prose or code. Full sentences are fine.\n\nPlan for about 45 minutes in total. If you run long, tell us where the time went and we will take that into account.\n\nReply to this email when you are done, or tell us if you need more time.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. In two or three sentences, describe how you would keep an embeddings pipeline correct when the embedding model is upgraded and the stored vectors must change. Cover how you would backfill existing rows and how you would avoid serving mixed-model results during the migration.\n\n2. Your team ingests about 5 million documents a day into Postgres with pgvector. Documents arrive in bursts, the embedding service rate-limits at 500 requests per second, and some documents fail to embed on the first attempt. Describe how you would design the embeddings pipeline so that ingestion keeps up, failures are retried without duplicate vectors, and the team can see how far behind the pipeline is at any moment. Name the main components and the failure modes you would watch for.\n\n3. Implement a JavaScript function solve(input) that takes a string and returns a string. The first line of input contains two integers k and o, the window size and the overlap. The second line contains whitespace-separated tokens. Produce overlapping windows of k tokens, where each new window starts (k - o) tokens after the previous one. Stop after the first window that reaches the final token. Join the tokens of each window with a single space and join the windows with ' | '. If there are no tokens, return an empty string. Assume 0 <= o < k.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793427246716},{"id":"msg_mvw09zzw_c122","runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","candidateId":"c122","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer, Retrieval Platform role","body":"Hello,\n\nThank you for applying for the Senior Backend Engineer role on the Retrieval Platform team. We are writing to move your application to the next step.\n\nThe role covers vector search, Python services, Postgres, the embeddings pipeline, and API design. We want to check a few areas that your profile does not show in detail.\n\nThe assessment has three tasks. The first is a short written question about Postgres indexing for vector search. The second is a scenario about designing a public search API. The third is a small JavaScript coding task with a function to write.\n\nPlease allow about 45 minutes. Answer in plain text or code in the reply to this email. You can take a short break between tasks if you need one.\n\nIf you have questions about the format, reply to this email.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. A table of 50 million document chunks stores embeddings in Postgres with pgvector. Queries filter by tenant_id and sort by cosine distance. Describe the index or indexes you would create, and explain one trade-off between recall and latency that you would tune.\n\n2. You are designing a public REST endpoint, POST /v1/searches, that accepts a query, filters, and a page size, and returns ranked results. Some results come from a slower reranking step that can time out. Describe the request and response shape, how you paginate, and what the client sees when reranking fails for part of the batch.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function solve(input) that takes a URL query string such as \"b=2&a=1&a=3\" (no leading ?). Return a canonical form in which the pairs are sorted by key, keys are compared as plain strings, and pairs with the same key keep their original relative order. Join the pairs with & and return the result as a string. An empty input returns an empty string.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793427246716},{"id":"msg_mvw09zzw_c002","runId":"run_mvw09e3zybn","candidateId":"c002","to":"test-inbox@example.com","subject":"Next step for the Senior Backend Engineer role, Retrieval Platform","body":"Hi,\n\nWe are hiring a Senior Backend Engineer for the Retrieval Platform team. The work covers vector search, embeddings pipelines, Postgres, and API design for internal and external clients.\n\nWe are writing because your profile did not show enough detail on API design, so we would like to see how you reason about it. The assessment has three tasks: one short written answer, one scenario question, and one small JavaScript coding task. Please allow about 45 minutes in total.\n\nReply to this email when you are finished. If you have questions about the format, ask before you start.\n\nThe hiring team\n\n---\n1. You are adding a search endpoint to a retrieval service. Clients need to page through results and filter by metadata. In a few sentences, describe the request and response shape you would use, and explain how you would handle pagination and filter validation so that the API stays stable as the schema changes.\n\n2. Your team owns a public API that returns similarity search results. Two downstream clients have started depending on a field name that you now want to rename, and a third client needs a new ranking score that changes the meaning of an existing field. Describe how you would roll out both changes without breaking current clients, including how you would communicate the timeline and how you would know when it is safe to remove the old behavior.\n\n3. Write a JavaScript function named solve(input) that takes a string containing a JSON array of query objects, each with fields \"id\" (string) and \"tags\" (array of strings). Return a string containing a JSON array of the ids whose tags include both \"public\" and \"indexed\", in the same order as the input. Return the result as JSON, for example [\"a\",\"c\"]. Return \"[]\" if none match.","channel":"outbox","status":"queued","ts":1793427246716}],"approvals":{"run_mvvyg9ub":{"candidateIds":["c178","c122"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793424189566},"run_mvvyhdgj":{"candidateIds":["c178","c122"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793424236180},"run_mvvysntsbki":{"candidateIds":["c178","c122"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793424829047},"run_mvvzdb13":{"candidateIds":["c156","c049","c066","c122"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793425768240},"run_mvvzhb93":{"candidateIds":["c049","c122","c156","c066"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793425952894},"run_mvvzl4ex":{"candidateIds":["c156","c122","c049","c066","c002"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793426121136},"prewarm_0_mvvzoa7p":{"candidateIds":["c156","c049","c066","c002","c122"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793426259354},"prewarm_1_mvvzprtg":{"candidateIds":["c002","c100","c156"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793426378040},"prewarm_2_mvvzrdvj":{"candidateIds":["c030","c041","c051","c171","c118"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793426418448},"run_mvw030g8":{"candidateIds":["c077","c020","c100"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793426965602},"run_mvw09e3zybn":{"candidateIds":["c049","c156","c066","c122","c002"],"removed":[],"approvedAt":1793427241075}}}